CC.provideCourse.bind(null,"c")({"lessons":{"c-01":{"html":"<p>A computer is very fast, but on its own it cannot think. A <strong>program</strong> is a step-by-step list of instructions written by a programmer so the computer knows exactly what to do.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-pipeline\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-main-parts-of-a-computer\"><h2 id=\"main-parts-of-a-computer\">Main parts of a computer</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Role</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CPU</strong> (Central Processing Unit)</td><td>The \"brain\" that executes instructions and does arithmetic</td><td>Intel Core / AMD Ryzen</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RAM</strong> (Memory)</td><td>Fast, temporary workspace for running programs and variables (erased when power is off)</td><td>8 GB / 16 GB RAM</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Storage</strong> (SSD / HDD)</td><td>Permanent storage for files, photos and programs even when turned off</td><td>512 GB SSD</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Input / Output (I/O)</strong></td><td>Devices that bring data in or show results out</td><td>Keyboard, mouse / screen, printer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-source-code-compiler-and-interpreter\"><h2 id=\"source-code-compiler-and-interpreter\">Source code, compiler and interpreter</h2>\n<p>Humans write programs in a <strong>high-level language</strong> like <strong>C</strong> or <strong>Python</strong>, called <strong>source code</strong> (<em>kaynak kod</em>). The CPU only understands binary <strong>machine code</strong> (<code>0</code>s and <code>1</code>s). There are two ways to translate:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Compiler (<em>derleyici</em>):</strong> Translates the <strong>entire</strong> C program into an executable machine-code file (e.g. <code>program.exe</code>) <em>before</em> you run it. If there is a syntax error, it refuses to build. <strong>C uses a compiler</strong> (such as <code>gcc</code>), which makes C programs very fast.</li><li><strong>Interpreter (<em>yorumlayıcı</em>):</strong> Reads and runs the code <strong>one line at a time</strong> while the program is executing (e.g. Python, JavaScript). Easier to test quickly, usually slower.</li></ul>\n<p>The C workflow is always: <strong>Write source (<code>main.c</code>) → Compile (<code>gcc main.c -o main</code>) → Run (<code>./main</code>)</strong>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-a-short-history-of-the-internet-it-basics\"><h2 id=\"a-short-history-of-the-internet-it-basics\">A short history of the internet (IT basics)</h2>\n<p>In the late 1960s, the US <strong>ARPANET</strong> connected four university computers to share research. In the 1970s–80s, <strong>TCP/IP</strong> protocols let many separate networks join together into the <strong>Internet</strong>. In 1989–1991, Tim Berners-Lee invented the <strong>World Wide Web</strong> (HTML, URLs, HTTP) on top of the Internet, turning it into the everyday information system we use today.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Even before learning C syntax, you can run your first C program right here! Press <strong>Run</strong> below, then try changing <code>\"Hello, IGU!\"</code> to print your own name.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"Hello, IGU!\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing <strong>RAM</strong> (temporary memory cleared when power turns off) with <strong>Storage</strong> (SSD/HDD, permanent).</li><li>Thinking the <strong>Internet</strong> and the <strong>World Wide Web</strong> are the exact same thing — the Internet is the global network of computers; the Web is one service (webpages) running on top of it.</li><li>Editing <code>.c</code> source code and forgetting to <strong>re-compile</strong> before running the executable again.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>program</strong></td><td>a step-by-step set of instructions for a computer to execute</td><td lang=\"tr\">program</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CPU</strong></td><td>Central Processing Unit — executes instructions and calculations</td><td lang=\"tr\">işlemci</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RAM</strong></td><td>fast temporary memory wiped when power is off</td><td lang=\"tr\">bellek (RAM)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>compiler</strong></td><td>translates the whole source file into machine code before running</td><td lang=\"tr\">derleyici</td></tr><tr><td><strong>interpreter</strong></td><td>translates and runs source code line by line</td><td lang=\"tr\">yorumlayıcı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What happens to data stored only in <strong>RAM</strong> when you turn the computer off?","opts":[{"h":"It moves to the CPU permanently","why":"The CPU has no permanent storage."},{"h":"It is lost (erased)","ok":1,"why":"RAM is volatile memory and needs power to keep its contents."},{"h":"It is saved to the SSD automatically","why":"Only files explicitly saved to storage remain."},{"h":"It stays in RAM forever","why":"Only non-volatile storage (SSD/HDD) keeps data without power."}],"why":"RAM is temporary working memory; permanent files live on storage (SSD/HDD)."},{"q":"How does a <strong>compiler</strong> differ from an <strong>interpreter</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"A compiler runs code line by line without saving an executable","why":"That describes an interpreter."},{"h":"A compiler translates the whole program into machine code <em>before</em> execution","ok":1,"why":"C uses a compiler (<code>gcc</code>), producing a fast executable file."},{"h":"An interpreter only works for C","why":"C is compiled; languages like Python are typically interpreted."},{"h":"A compiler is hardware inside the monitor","why":"A compiler is software."}],"why":"C is a compiled language: source code → compiler → machine code → run."},{"q":"Which 1960s network was the ancestor of today's Internet?","opts":[{"h":"World Wide Web","why":"The Web was invented in 1989–1991 on top of the Internet."},{"h":"ARPANET","ok":1,"why":"ARPANET connected the first university research nodes in 1969."},{"h":"Bluetooth","why":"Bluetooth is a short-range wireless standard from the 1990s."},{"h":"Ethernet","why":"Ethernet is a local wired LAN technology."}],"why":"ARPANET evolved into the global Internet after adopting TCP/IP."}]],"id":"c-01","title":"What a program is and how computers work","goal":"explain what a computer program is, how compilers differ from interpreters, and what the main computer parts do","week":"1","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"program","m":"a step-by-step set of instructions for a computer to execute","tr":"program"},{"t":"CPU","m":"Central Processing Unit — executes instructions and calculations","tr":"işlemci"},{"t":"RAM","m":"fast temporary memory wiped when power is off","tr":"bellek (RAM)"},{"t":"compiler","m":"translates the whole source file into machine code before running","tr":"derleyici"},{"t":"interpreter","m":"translates and runs source code line by line","tr":"yorumlayıcı"}]},"c-02":{"html":"<p>Good programmers do not start by typing C code. First they solve the problem in plain steps. An <strong>algorithm</strong> (<em>algoritma</em>) is a finite, unambiguous, step-by-step recipe that takes <strong>input</strong>, processes it, and produces <strong>output</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-rules-of-a-good-algorithm\"><h2 id=\"rules-of-a-good-algorithm\">Rules of a good algorithm</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Clear steps:</strong> No vague instructions like <em>\"do something with x\"</em>.</li><li><strong>Exact order:</strong> Steps run in sequence unless a decision or loop jumps.</li><li><strong>Finite:</strong> It must eventually stop and produce a result.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-plain-language-vs-pseudocode\"><h2 id=\"plain-language-vs-pseudocode\">Plain language vs pseudocode</h2>\n<p><strong>Pseudocode</strong> (<em>sözde kod</em>) looks halfway between English and a programming language. There is no strict syntax — the goal is clarity.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Find the larger of two numbers <code>A</code> and <code>B</code>.</p>\n<p>In plain steps:</p>\n<ol><li>Start.</li><li>Read two numbers <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> from the user.</li><li>If <code>A &gt; B</code>, print <code>A</code>. Otherwise, print <code>B</code>.</li><li>Stop.</li></ol>\n<p>In pseudocode:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>START\n  INPUT A, B\n  IF A &gt; B THEN\n      OUTPUT A\n  ELSE\n      OUTPUT B\n  END IF\nSTOP</code></pre>\n<p>Notice how easy it is to turn that into C once you know the syntax:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\" data-stdin=\"14 9\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &amp;a, &amp;b);\n    if (a &gt; b) {\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    } else {\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", b);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write a step-by-step algorithm (on paper) that reads a student's score <code>0..100</code> and prints <code>\"Pass\"</code> if <code>score &gt;= 50</code>, or <code>\"Fail\"</code> otherwise. Then open the model pseudocode to compare.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model pseudocode</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>START\n  INPUT score\n  IF score &gt;= 50 THEN\n      OUTPUT \"Pass\"\n  ELSE\n      OUTPUT \"Fail\"\n  END IF\nSTOP</code></pre></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing an algorithm that never stops (e.g. a loop whose counter never changes).</li><li>Skipping the <code>ELSE</code> case when the problem asks for two outcomes.</li><li>Worrying about semicolons <code>;</code> or <code>#include</code> in pseudocode — pseudocode is for logic, not compiler syntax.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>algorithm</strong></td><td>a finite, unambiguous step-by-step procedure to solve a problem</td><td lang=\"tr\">algoritma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>pseudocode</strong></td><td>informal code-like notation for writing an algorithm</td><td lang=\"tr\">sözde kod</td></tr><tr><td><strong>input</strong></td><td>data given to a program from the user or a file</td><td lang=\"tr\">girdi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>output</strong></td><td>the result produced and displayed by a program</td><td lang=\"tr\">çıktı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the main purpose of <strong>pseudocode</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"To be compiled directly by <code>gcc</code>","why":"Compilers cannot compile pseudocode."},{"h":"To plan and communicate an algorithm clearly without worrying about exact language syntax","ok":1,"why":"You design the logic first, then translate to C."},{"h":"To replace RAM","why":"Pseudocode is a notation, not hardware."},{"h":"To draw hardware circuits","why":"That is circuit design."}],"why":"Pseudocode bridges human thinking and C code."},{"q":"Which property is <strong>required</strong> for every valid algorithm?","opts":[{"h":"It must be written in C","why":"Algorithms can be written in plain English, pseudocode, flowcharts or any language."},{"h":"It must have clear, unambiguous steps and finish in a finite number of steps","ok":1,"why":"Infinite or vague procedures are not valid algorithms."},{"h":"It must use at least 10 variables","why":"Use only the variables the problem needs."},{"h":"It must never take input","why":"Many algorithms take input, though some have fixed data."}],"why":"Every algorithm must be clear, ordered and finite."},{"q":"Trace this algorithm when <code>N = 4</code>: <code>sum = 0; FOR i = 1 TO N: sum = sum + i; OUTPUT sum</code>. What does it print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>4</code>","why":"It adds <code>1 + 2 + 3 + 4</code>, not just <code>N</code>."},{"h":"<code>6</code>","why":"<code>1 + 2 + 3 = 6</code>, missing <code>i = 4</code>."},{"h":"<code>10</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10</code>."},{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"<code>sum</code> increases on every iteration."}],"why":"Tracing step by step: <code>0 + 1 = 1</code>, <code>+ 2 = 3</code>, <code>+ 3 = 6</code>, <code>+ 4 = 10</code>."}]],"id":"c-02","title":"Algorithms and pseudocode","goal":"write clear step-by-step algorithms in plain English and pseudocode before writing C code","week":"2","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"algorithm","m":"a finite, unambiguous step-by-step procedure to solve a problem","tr":"algoritma"},{"t":"pseudocode","m":"informal code-like notation for writing an algorithm","tr":"sözde kod"},{"t":"input","m":"data given to a program from the user or a file","tr":"girdi"},{"t":"output","m":"the result produced and displayed by a program","tr":"çıktı"}]},"c-03":{"html":"<p>A <strong>flowchart</strong> (<em>akış şeması</em>) is a visual map of an algorithm. Instead of reading paragraphs, you follow arrows from <strong>Start</strong> to <strong>End</strong>. Flowcharts appear very often in Programming Basics I written exams!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-symbols\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-5-standard-symbols\"><h2 id=\"the-5-standard-symbols\">The 5 standard symbols</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Shape</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Terminal</strong></td><td>Rounded pill / oval</td><td><code>Start</code> or <code>End</code> of the program</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Input / Output</strong></td><td>Parallelogram (slanted box)</td><td><code>Read n</code> or <code>Print result</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Process</strong></td><td>Rectangle</td><td>Calculation or assignment (<code>sum = a + b</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Decision</strong></td><td>Diamond</td><td>Yes/No (<code>True</code>/<code>False</code>) question (<code>n &gt; 0 ?</code>) — always has <strong>two</strong> outgoing arrows</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Flow line</strong></td><td>Arrow <code>→</code></td><td>Shows which step runs next</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example-even-or-odd\"><h2 id=\"worked-example-even-or-odd\">Worked example: even or odd</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read n\nd   dec    n % 2 == 0 ?  yes:e  no:o\ne   io     Print \"Even\"  -&gt;x\no   io     Print \"Odd\"  @right\nx   end    End</pre></div>\n<p>Follow the arrows:</p>\n<ol><li>Start → Read <code>n</code> (parallelogram).</li><li>Decision diamond asks <code>n % 2 == 0 ?</code> - If <strong>Yes</strong>, go down to <code>Print \"Even\"</code>, then to <code>End</code>. - If <strong>No</strong>, go right to <code>Print \"Odd\"</code>, then down to <code>End</code>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Draw a flowchart on paper to read a number <code>x</code>, multiply it by <code>2</code> (<code>y = x * 2</code>), and print <code>y</code>. Then check the model flowchart below.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model flowchart</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read x\np   proc   y = x * 2\no   io     Print y\nx   end    End</pre></div></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting a calculation like <code>sum = a + b</code> inside a parallelogram (that is <strong>Process</strong> = rectangle, not Input/Output).</li><li>Leaving a <strong>Decision</strong> diamond with only 1 outgoing arrow — every decision must have <strong>both</strong> a <code>Yes</code> (True) and a <code>No</code> (False) branch.</li><li>Using a rectangle for <code>Start</code> or <code>End</code> instead of the rounded terminal shape.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>flowchart</strong></td><td>a diagram showing the step-by-step flow of an algorithm</td><td lang=\"tr\">akış şeması</td></tr><tr><td><strong>terminal symbol</strong></td><td>rounded oval marking Start or End</td><td lang=\"tr\">başla/bitir sembolü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>decision symbol</strong></td><td>diamond shape branching on a Yes/No condition</td><td lang=\"tr\">karar sembolü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>process symbol</strong></td><td>rectangle shape representing a calculation or assignment</td><td lang=\"tr\">işlem sembolü</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which flowchart shape is used for <strong>reading input</strong> or <strong>printing output</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Rectangle","why":"Rectangles are for processing/calculations."},{"h":"Parallelogram","ok":1,"why":"Slanted parallelograms represent Input/Output."},{"h":"Diamond","why":"Diamonds are for Yes/No decisions."},{"h":"Rounded oval","why":"Ovals mark Start and End."}],"why":"Parallelogram = I/O; Rectangle = Process; Diamond = Decision; Oval = Start/End."},{"q":"How many outgoing arrows must a <strong>Decision</strong> diamond have?","opts":[{"h":"Always 1","why":"With 1 arrow, there is no decision!"},{"h":"Always 2 (Yes/True and No/False)","ok":1,"why":"A condition is either true or false."},{"h":"3 or more","why":"Standard flowcharts use binary Yes/No diamonds."},{"h":"0","why":"Only <code>End</code> has 0 outgoing arrows."}],"why":"Every decision diamond branches into <code>Yes</code> and <code>No</code>."},{"q":"Which shape should hold <code>total = price * qty</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Parallelogram","why":"That is for reading or printing, not calculating."},{"h":"Rectangle (Process)","ok":1,"why":"Assignments and arithmetic belong in a rectangle."},{"h":"Diamond","why":"<code>total = price * qty</code> is not a Yes/No question."},{"h":"Oval","why":"Ovals are only for Start and End."}],"why":"Calculations and variable updates use the Process rectangle."}]],"id":"c-03","title":"Flowchart symbols","goal":"recognize and use the standard flowchart shapes (terminal, process, input/output, decision, flow arrows)","week":"2","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"flowchart","m":"a diagram showing the step-by-step flow of an algorithm","tr":"akış şeması"},{"t":"terminal symbol","m":"rounded oval marking Start or End","tr":"başla/bitir sembolü"},{"t":"decision symbol","m":"diamond shape branching on a Yes/No condition","tr":"karar sembolü"},{"t":"process symbol","m":"rectangle shape representing a calculation or assignment","tr":"işlem sembolü"}]},"c-04":{"html":"<p>Week 3 of the syllabus focuses on classic flowchart problems that appear directly on written exams. Study each pattern below: <strong>sequence</strong>, <strong>branching</strong>, and <strong>loops</strong> (arrows pointing back up!).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-1-sum-of-two-numbers\"><h2 id=\"1-sum-of-two-numbers\">1. Sum of two numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read a, b\np   proc   sum = a + b\no   io     Print sum\nx   end    End</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-larger-of-two-numbers\"><h2 id=\"2-larger-of-two-numbers\">2. Larger of two numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read a, b\nd   dec    a &gt; b ?  yes:pa  no:pb\npa  io     Print a  -&gt;x\npb  io     Print b  @right\nx   end    End</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-largest-of-three-numbers-a-b-c\"><h2 id=\"3-largest-of-three-numbers-a-b-c\">3. Largest of three numbers (<code>a, b, c</code>)</h2>\n<p>Notice the clean pattern: start by assuming <code>max = a</code>, then check <code>b</code> and <code>c</code> in sequence!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read a, b, c\np0  proc   max = a\nd1  dec    b &gt; max ?  yes:u1  no:d2\nu1  proc   max = b  @right  -&gt;d2\nd2  dec    c &gt; max ?  yes:u2  no:o\nu2  proc   max = c  @right  -&gt;o\no   io     Print max\nx   end    End</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-sum-of-1-n-looping-flowchart\"><h2 id=\"4-sum-of-1-n-looping-flowchart\">4. Sum of <code>1 .. N</code> (looping flowchart)</h2>\n<p>Watch how the arrow jumps <strong>back up</strong> to <code>d</code> until <code>i &lt;= N</code> becomes false:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read N\np0  proc   sum = 0, i = 1\nd   dec    i &lt;= N ?  yes:body  no:out\nbody proc  sum = sum + i; i = i + 1  -&gt;d\nout io     Print sum  @right\nx   end    End</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-5-factorial-n-1-times-2-times-dots-times-n\"><h2 id=\"5-factorial-n-1-times-2-times-dots-times-n\">5. Factorial <code>N!</code> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"1 \\times 2 \\times \\dots \\times N\"></span>)</h2>\n<p>Exact same loop shape — just start <code>fact = 1</code> and multiply instead of adding!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read N\np0  proc   fact = 1, i = 1\nd   dec    i &lt;= N ?  yes:body  no:out\nbody proc  fact = fact * i; i = i + 1  -&gt;d\nout io     Print fact  @right\nx   end    End</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Take paper and a pen and draw the flowchart for each problem below, then open the model flowchart to compare.</p>\n<h3>Task A: Grade letter from score (<code>&gt;= 90</code> A, <code>&gt;= 70</code> B, <code>&gt;= 50</code> C, else F)</h3>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model flowchart (Grade letter)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr   io     Read score\nd1  dec    score &gt;= 90 ?  yes:ga  no:d2\nga  io     Print \"A\"  @right  -&gt;x\nd2  dec    score &gt;= 70 ?  yes:gb  no:d3\ngb  io     Print \"B\"  @right  -&gt;x\nd3  dec    score &gt;= 50 ?  yes:gc  no:gf\ngc  io     Print \"C\"  @right  -&gt;x\ngf  io     Print \"F\"\nx   end    End</pre></div></div></details>\n<h3>Task B: Maximum in a list of <code>N</code> numbers</h3>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model flowchart (Max in a list)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s   start  Start\nr0  io     Read N, first num -&gt; max\np0  proc   i = 2\nd1  dec    i &lt;= N ?  yes:r1  no:out\nr1  io     Read x\nd2  dec    x &gt; max ?  yes:up  no:inc\nup  proc   max = x  @right  -&gt;inc\ninc proc   i = i + 1  -&gt;d1\nout io     Print max  @right\nx   end    End</pre></div></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Initializing <code>fact = 0</code> instead of <code>fact = 1</code> — multiplying by <code>0</code> makes the answer always <code>0</code>!</li><li>Initializing <code>max = 0</code> when finding the maximum of a list — if all input numbers are negative (e.g. <code>-5, -2, -9</code>), <code>0</code> would wrongly win! Always initialize <code>max</code> to the <strong>first number</strong>.</li><li>Forgetting <code>i = i + 1</code> inside a loop flowchart, creating an infinite loop.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>loop</strong></td><td>repeating a block of steps while a condition stays true</td><td lang=\"tr\">döngü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>counter variable</strong></td><td>a variable (like <code>i</code>) that tracks how many times a loop has run</td><td lang=\"tr\">sayaç değişkeni</td></tr><tr><td><strong>accumulator</strong></td><td>a variable (like <code>sum</code> or <code>fact</code>) that builds up a total step by step</td><td lang=\"tr\">biriktirici değişken</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"In the <code>1 .. N</code> sum flowchart, what happens if you forget <code>i = i + 1</code>?","opts":[{"h":"It prints <code>N</code>","why":"It never reaches the <code>Print</code> step."},{"h":"The condition <code>i &lt;= N</code> stays true forever (infinite loop)","ok":1,"why":"<code>i</code> remains <code>1</code> on every step."},{"h":"It computes <code>N!</code>","why":"Multiplying computes <code>N!</code>, not forgetting the increment."},{"h":"It stops after 1 step","why":"Without incrementing <code>i</code>, the loop never ends."}],"why":"Every loop needs an update step (<code>i = i + 1</code>) that moves toward the exit condition."},{"q":"When finding the <strong>largest</strong> number in a list of <code>N</code> user-entered numbers, what should <code>max</code> be initialized to?","opts":[{"h":"Always <code>0</code>","why":"Fails if all numbers are negative!"},{"h":"Always <code>100</code>","why":"Fails if all numbers are smaller than 100!"},{"h":"The <strong>first</strong> number read from the user","ok":1,"why":"Guaranteed to be a real value from the list."},{"h":"<code>N</code>","why":"<code>N</code> is the count of numbers, not one of the values."}],"why":"Initialize <code>max</code> (and <code>min</code>) to the first element of the list."},{"q":"In the factorial flowchart, what must <code>fact</code> start at before the loop?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"<code>0 * 1 * 2 ... = 0</code>."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>1</code> is the identity element for multiplication (and <code>0! = 1</code>)."},{"h":"<code>N</code>","why":"Starting at <code>N</code> and multiplying <code>1..N</code> would compute <code>N * N!</code>."},{"h":"<code>-1</code>","why":"Would make the result negative."}],"why":"Always initialize a sum accumulator to <code>0</code> and a product/factorial accumulator to <code>1</code>."}]],"id":"c-04","title":"Classic flowchart examples","goal":"read and draw flowcharts for sums, max/min, grade letters, factorial and loops","week":"3","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"loop","m":"repeating a block of steps while a condition stays true","tr":"döngü"},{"t":"counter variable","m":"a variable (like `i`) that tracks how many times a loop has run","tr":"sayaç değişkeni"},{"t":"accumulator","m":"a variable (like `sum` or `fact`) that builds up a total step by step","tr":"biriktirici değişken"}]},"c-05":{"html":"<p>Every C program has the same basic skeleton. Once you learn it, you can focus on solving problems.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-skeleton\"></div>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-variables\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-anatomy-of-a-c-program\"><h2 id=\"anatomy-of-a-c-program\">Anatomy of a C program</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;   // gives us printf and scanf\n\n/* Every C program starts running inside main() */\nint main() {\n    int age = 19;\n    printf(\"Age is %d\\n\", age);\n    return 0;        // 0 tells the OS the program finished without errors\n}</pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;</code> loads the <strong>Standard Input/Output</strong> library (<code>printf</code>, <code>scanf</code>).</li><li><code>int main() { ... }</code> is the entry point — execution always starts at <code>main</code>.</li><li>Every statement ends with a semicolon <code>;</code>.</li><li><strong>Comments:</strong> <code>// single-line</code> and <code>/* multi-line */</code> are ignored by the compiler.</li><li><strong>Compiling on your computer with gcc:</strong> <code>gcc hello.c -o hello</code> then <code>./hello</code> (or <code>hello.exe</code> on Windows).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-variables-and-the-4-core-data-types\"><h2 id=\"variables-and-the-4-core-data-types\">Variables and the 4 core data types</h2>\n<p>A <strong>variable</strong> (<em>değişken</em>) is a named box in RAM that holds one value of a specific <strong>data type</strong> (<em>veri tipi</em>).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Stores</th><th>Typical size (<code>sizeof</code>)</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>Whole numbers (positive, negative, 0)</td><td>4 bytes</td><td><code>int count = -12;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>Decimal numbers (~6–7 digits precision)</td><td>4 bytes</td><td><code>float gpa = 3.45f;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>double</code></td><td>High-precision decimals (~15 digits)</td><td>8 bytes</td><td><code>double pi = 3.1415926535;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>char</code></td><td>A single character (in single quotes <code>' '</code>)</td><td>1 byte</td><td><code>char grade = 'A';</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Variable naming rules:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Use letters, digits and <code>_</code> only (never spaces, <code>-</code>, or Turkish characters like <code>ş, ğ</code>).</li><li><strong>Cannot start with a digit</strong> (<code>1num</code> is invalid; <code>num1</code> is valid).</li><li>Cannot be a C keyword (<code>int</code>, <code>return</code>, <code>if</code>).</li><li>C is <strong>case-sensitive</strong> (<code>Age</code> and <code>age</code> are two different variables).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Declaration vs initialization:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Declaration: <code>int x;</code> (box created, value is <strong>uninitialized garbage</strong> — never read it before assigning!).</li><li>Initialization: <code>int x = 10;</code> (created with a known starting value).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int year = 2026;\n    float price = 49.90f;\n    double exact = 3.14159265;\n    char letter = 'C';\n\n    printf(\"year=%d, price=%.2f, exact=%.6f, letter=%c\\n\", year, price, exact, letter);\n    printf(\"Sizes (bytes): int=%d float=%d double=%d char=%d\\n\",\n           (int)sizeof(int), (int)sizeof(float), (int)sizeof(double), (int)sizeof(char));\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the exercises for this lesson below (start with easy, then try medium and exam-level).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-05\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the semicolon <code>;</code> at the end of a statement.</li><li>Using double quotes <code>\"A\"</code> for a <code>char</code> instead of single quotes <code>'A'</code> (<code>\"A\"</code> is a string, <code>'A'</code> is a <code>char</code>).</li><li>Reading a declared variable before giving it a value (<code>int x; printf(\"%d\", x);</code> prints garbage!).</li><li>Starting a variable name with a number like <code>2nd_score</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;</strong></td><td>preprocessor directive loading standard I/O functions</td><td lang=\"tr\">standart G/Ç başlığı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>variable</strong></td><td>a named memory location holding a value of a given type</td><td lang=\"tr\">değişken</td></tr><tr><td><strong>initialization</strong></td><td>giving a variable its first value when declaring it</td><td lang=\"tr\">ilk değer atama</td></tr><tr><td><strong>sizeof</strong></td><td>operator that returns the memory size of a type/variable in bytes</td><td lang=\"tr\">boyut operatörü</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which variable name is <strong>valid</strong> in C?","opts":[{"h":"<code>1student</code>","why":"Variable names cannot start with a digit."},{"h":"<code>student-age</code>","why":"Hyphens <code>-</code> are subtraction, not allowed in names."},{"h":"<code>return</code>","why":"<code>return</code> is a reserved C keyword."},{"h":"<code>student_age_1</code>","ok":1,"why":"Starts with a letter and uses only letters, digits and <code>_</code>."}],"why":"C identifiers use letters, digits and <code>_</code>, and cannot start with a digit or be a keyword."},{"q":"Which data type is best for storing a single letter <code>'K'</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>int</code>","why":"<code>int</code> is for whole numbers (though <code>char</code> is a small integer underneath)."},{"h":"<code>float</code>","why":"<code>float</code> is for decimal numbers."},{"h":"<code>char</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>char</code> holds a single character in 1 byte."},{"h":"<code>double</code>","why":"<code>double</code> is for high-precision decimals."}],"why":"Use <code>char</code> with single quotes <code>'K'</code> for a single character."},{"q":"What happens if you forget <code>;</code> at the end of <code>int x = 5</code> in C?","opts":[{"h":"The program still runs normally","why":"C requires <code>;</code> to terminate every statement."},{"h":"The compiler stops with a syntax error","ok":1,"why":"C compilers refuse to build code with missing semicolons."},{"h":"<code>x</code> becomes <code>0</code>","why":"It will not compile at all."},{"h":"Only a warning is printed","why":"Missing <code>;</code> is a hard compile error."}],"why":"Every statement in C must end with <code>;</code>."}]],"id":"c-05","title":"Intro to C and data types","goal":"write a basic C program, compile it with gcc, and choose between int, float, double and char","week":"4","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"#include <stdio.h>","m":"preprocessor directive loading standard I/O functions","tr":"standart G/Ç başlığı"},{"t":"variable","m":"a named memory location holding a value of a given type","tr":"değişken"},{"t":"initialization","m":"giving a variable its first value when declaring it","tr":"ilk değer atama"},{"t":"sizeof","m":"operator that returns the memory size of a type/variable in bytes","tr":"boyut operatörü"}]},"c-06":{"html":"<p>Programs become interactive when they display results (<code>printf</code>) and read values typed by the user (<code>scanf</code>). Both live in <code>&lt;stdio.h&gt;</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-io\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-printf-and-format-specifiers\"><h2 id=\"printf-and-format-specifiers\"><code>printf</code> and format specifiers</h2>\n<p>A <strong>format specifier</strong> (<em>biçim belirteci</em>) is a placeholder starting with <code>%</code> inside the quotes of <code>printf</code>, telling C what type of value to print there:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Specifier</th><th>Type</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>%d</code></td><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>printf(\"%d\", 42);</code> → <code>42</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%f</code></td><td><code>float</code> or <code>double</code></td><td><code>printf(\"%.2f\", 3.14159);</code> → <code>3.14</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%c</code></td><td><code>char</code></td><td><code>printf(\"%c\", 'A');</code> → <code>A</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%s</code></td><td>string (<code>char[]</code>)</td><td><code>printf(\"%s\", \"IGU\");</code> → <code>IGU</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Escape sequences</strong> start with <code>\\</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>\\n</code> = new line</li><li><code>\\t</code> = tab (align columns)</li><li><code>\\\\</code> = literal backslash, <code>\\\"</code> = literal quote, <code>%%</code> = literal <code>%</code> sign</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-scanf-and-the-address-of-operator\"><h2 id=\"scanf-and-the-address-of-operator\"><code>scanf</code> and the <code>&amp;</code> (address-of) operator</h2>\n<p><code>scanf</code> reads input from the keyboard and stores it into a variable. Because <code>scanf</code> needs to know <strong>where in memory</strong> your variable lives, you must put <code>&amp;</code> (<em>address-of</em>) in front of <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>double</code> and <code>char</code> variables!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\" data-stdin=\"20 3.75\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int age;\n    double gpa;\n\n    scanf(\"%d %lf\", &amp;age, &amp;gpa);\n    printf(\"Age: %d\\nGPA: %.2f\\n\", age, gpa);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Watch out: <code>float</code> vs <code>double</code> in <code>scanf</code>!</div><ul><li>In <code>printf</code>, <code>%f</code> prints <strong>both</strong> <code>float</code> and <code>double</code>.</li><li>In <code>scanf</code>, <strong><code>%f</code> is ONLY for <code>float</code></strong>, and <strong><code>%lf</code> is for <code>double</code></strong>! Using <code>%f</code> for a <code>double</code> in <code>scanf</code> corrupts the value.</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Reading a <code>char</code> with <code>scanf</code></div><p>When you read a number then a <code>char</code>, the <code>Enter</code> key (<code>\\n</code>) left in the input buffer gets swallowed by <code>%c</code>! Put a <strong>space before <code>%c</code></strong> (<code>scanf(\" %c\", &amp;ch);</code>) to skip leftover whitespace and newlines.</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Practice <code>printf</code> and <code>scanf</code> in the exercises below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-06\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting <code>&amp;</code> in <code>scanf(\"%d\", x);</code> — one of the most common C crashes/bugs!</li><li>Using <code>%f</code> instead of <code>%lf</code> when reading a <code>double</code> with <code>scanf</code>.</li><li>Writing <code>scanf(\"%d\\n\", &amp;x);</code> — never put <code>\\n</code> inside <code>scanf</code>'s format string (it waits for extra non-whitespace input).</li><li>Passing arguments in the wrong order or mismatching <code>%d</code> with a <code>float</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>printf</strong></td><td>prints formatted text and values to the screen</td><td lang=\"tr\">ekrana yazdırma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>scanf</strong></td><td>reads formatted input from the keyboard into variables</td><td lang=\"tr\">klavyeden okuma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>format specifier</strong></td><td>placeholder like <code>%d</code>, <code>%f</code>, <code>%c</code>, <code>%s</code> matching a data type</td><td lang=\"tr\">biçim belirteci</td></tr><tr><td><strong>escape sequence</strong></td><td>special character starting with <code>\\</code> such as <code>\\n</code> or <code>\\t</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">kaçış dizisi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is wrong with <code>int n; scanf(\"%d\", n);</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>n</code> must be a <code>float</code>","why":"<code>%d</code> matches <code>int</code>."},{"h":"Missing <code>&amp;</code> before <code>n</code> (<code>&amp;n</code>)","ok":1,"why":"<code>scanf</code> needs the memory address of <code>n</code>."},{"h":"Missing <code>\\n</code> inside <code>scanf</code>","why":"You should NOT put <code>\\n</code> inside <code>scanf</code>."},{"h":"<code>scanf</code> cannot read <code>int</code>","why":"<code>scanf</code> reads <code>int</code> with <code>%d</code>."}],"why":"Always pass <code>&amp;var</code> to <code>scanf</code> for <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>double</code>, and <code>char</code>."},{"q":"Which specifier must you use in <code>scanf</code> to read into a <code>double x;</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>%d</code>","why":"<code>%d</code> is for <code>int</code>."},{"h":"<code>%f</code>","why":"<code>%f</code> in <code>scanf</code> expects a 4-byte <code>float*</code>, not an 8-byte <code>double*</code>."},{"h":"<code>%lf</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>double</code> requires <code>%lf</code> in <code>scanf</code>."},{"h":"<code>%c</code>","why":"<code>%c</code> is for <code>char</code>."}],"why":"Use <code>%lf</code> for <code>double</code> in <code>scanf</code> (and <code>%f</code> or <code>%.2f</code> in <code>printf</code>)."},{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"A\\tB\\nC%%\");</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>A t B n C%</code>","why":"<code>\\t</code> and <code>\\n</code> are tab and newline, not letters."},{"h":"<code>A</code> + tab + <code>B</code>, then <code>C%</code> on the next line","ok":1,"why":"<code>\\t</code> = tab, <code>\\n</code> = newline, <code>%%</code> = literal <code>%</code>."},{"h":"<code>A\\tB\\nC%%</code>","why":"Escape sequences are interpreted, not printed literally."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"All escape sequences here are valid."}],"why":"<code>\\t</code> inserts a tab, <code>\\n</code> starts a new line, and <code>%%</code> prints one <code>%</code>."}]],"id":"c-06","title":"Basic input and output: printf and scanf","goal":"display formatted values with printf and read user input safely with scanf and &","week":"5","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"printf","m":"prints formatted text and values to the screen","tr":"ekrana yazdırma"},{"t":"scanf","m":"reads formatted input from the keyboard into variables","tr":"klavyeden okuma"},{"t":"format specifier","m":"placeholder like `%d`, `%f`, `%c`, `%s` matching a data type","tr":"biçim belirteci"},{"t":"escape sequence","m":"special character starting with `\\` such as `\\n` or `\\t`","tr":"kaçış dizisi"}]},"c-07":{"html":"<p>Operators are the symbols that do math, compare values, and combine conditions. Written midterm exams love \"predict the output\" questions on <strong>integer division</strong> and <strong><code>++</code>/<code>--</code></strong>!</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-1-arithmetic-operators-and-integer-division\"><h2 id=\"1-arithmetic-operators-and-integer-division\">1. Arithmetic operators and integer division</h2>\n<p><code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>%</code> (modulo = remainder of integer division, e.g. <code>17 % 5 == 2</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Integer division trap!</div><p>In C, <strong><code>int / int</code> ALWAYS produces an <code>int</code></strong> — the decimal part is chopped off (truncated toward zero)!</p>\n<ul><li><code>7 / 2</code> is <strong><code>3</code></strong>, NOT <code>3.5</code>!</li><li><code>double x = 7 / 2;</code> still gives <code>3.0</code>, because <code>7 / 2</code> happened as integers <em>before</em> assigning to <code>x</code>!</li><li>To get <code>3.5</code>, make at least one side floating-point: <strong><code>(double)7 / 2</code></strong> or <strong><code>7 / 2.0</code></strong>.</li></ul></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-assignment-and-prefix-vs-postfix\"><h2 id=\"2-assignment-and-prefix-vs-postfix\">2. Assignment and <code>++</code> / <code>--</code> (prefix vs postfix)</h2>\n<p>Compound assignment: <code>x += 5;</code> means <code>x = x + 5;</code> (same for <code>-=</code>, <code>*=</code>, <code>/=</code>, <code>%=</code>).</p>\n<p>Increment <code>++</code> adds 1; decrement <code>--</code> subtracts 1. Watch <strong>where</strong> the <code>++</code> sits:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Prefix (<code>++i</code>):</strong> add 1 <strong>first</strong>, <em>then</em> use the new value.</li><li><strong>Postfix (<code>i++</code>):</strong> use the <strong>current</strong> value <em>first</em>, <em>then</em> add 1.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int i = 5;\n    int a = i++;   // a gets 5, then i becomes 6\n    int b = ++i;   // i becomes 7, then b gets 7\n    printf(\"i=%d a=%d b=%d\\n\", i, a, b);\n\n    printf(\"7/2 = %d, 7/2.0 = %.1f, 17%%5 = %d\\n\", 7 / 2, 7 / 2.0, 17 % 5);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-relational-and-logical-operators\"><h2 id=\"3-relational-and-logical-operators\">3. Relational and logical operators</h2>\n<p>Relational operators return <code>1</code> (true) or <code>0</code> (false): <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>. Logical operators combine conditions:</p>\n<ul><li><code>&amp;&amp;</code> (<strong>AND</strong>): true only if <em>both</em> sides are true.</li><li><code>||</code> (<strong>OR</strong>): true if <em>at least one</em> side is true.</li><li><code>!</code> (<strong>NOT</strong>): flips true (<code>1</code>) ↔ false (<code>0</code>).</li></ul>\n<p><em>(Short-circuiting: in <code>a &amp;&amp; b</code>, if <code>a</code> is false, <code>b</code> is not even evaluated. In <code>a || b</code>, if <code>a</code> is true, <code>b</code> is skipped.)</em></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-operator-precedence-highest-lowest\"><h2 id=\"4-operator-precedence-highest-lowest\">4. Operator precedence (highest → lowest)</h2>\n<ol><li><code>( )</code></li><li>Unary: <code>!</code>, <code>++</code>, <code>--</code>, unary <code>-</code>, <code>(type)</code> cast</li><li><code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>%</code></li><li><code>+</code>, <code>-</code></li><li><code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code></li><li><code>==</code>, <code>!=</code></li><li><code>&amp;&amp;</code></li><li><code>||</code></li><li><code>=</code>, <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code>, etc.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Work through the predict-the-output, bug-hunt and coding exercises below!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-07\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing <code>=</code> (assignment) instead of <code>==</code> (comparison) inside <code>if (x = 5)</code> — that sets <code>x</code> to 5 and is always true!</li><li>Writing <code>1 &lt;= x &lt;= 10</code> in C — C evaluates <code>(1 &lt;= x)</code> as <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>, then compares that to <code>10</code> (always true!). Always write <code>x &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; x &lt;= 10</code>.</li><li>Using <code>%</code> with <code>float</code> or <code>double</code> — <code>%</code> only works on integers (<code>int</code>).</li><li>Forgetting <code>(double)</code> cast when dividing two <code>int</code> variables for an average.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>integer division</strong></td><td>division of two integers that truncates any remainder</td><td lang=\"tr\">tam sayı bölmesi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>modulo (%)</strong></td><td>returns the remainder of integer division</td><td lang=\"tr\">mod (kalan) operatörü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>type casting</strong></td><td>converting a value to another type, e.g. <code>(double)a / b</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">tür dönüşümü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>short-circuit evaluation</strong></td><td>skipping the right side of <code>&amp;&amp;</code> or <code>||</code> once the result is already known</td><td lang=\"tr\">kısa devre değerlendirme</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"%.1f\\n\", (float)(9 / 2));</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>4.5</code>","why":"<code>9 / 2</code> is evaluated <em>inside</em> parentheses as <code>int / int = 4</code> before the <code>(float)</code> cast!"},{"h":"<code>4.0</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>9 / 2</code> gives integer <code>4</code>, then <code>(float)4</code> prints as <code>4.0</code>."},{"h":"<code>5.0</code>","why":"Integer division truncates toward zero; it never rounds up."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","why":"<code>%.1f</code> prints one decimal place."}],"why":"Parentheses force <code>9 / 2</code> to happen as integer division first (<code>4</code>), then cast to <code>4.0</code>."},{"q":"After <code>int x = 3; int y = x++ * 2;</code>, what are <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>x = 4, y = 8</code>","why":"<code>x++</code> is postfix, so <code>y</code> uses the old value <code>3</code>."},{"h":"<code>x = 4, y = 6</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>y</code> gets <code>3 * 2 = 6</code>, and <code>x</code> increments to <code>4</code>."},{"h":"<code>x = 3, y = 6</code>","why":"<code>x</code> still increments to <code>4</code>."},{"h":"<code>x = 4, y = 7</code>","why":"<code>x</code> increments by 1 after the multiplication."}],"why":"Postfix <code>x++</code> returns the old value (<code>3</code>) for the expression, then increments <code>x</code> to <code>4</code>."},{"q":"How do you write \"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span> is between 10 and 20 inclusive\" in C?","opts":[{"h":"<code>10 &lt;= x &lt;= 20</code>","why":"In C, <code>(10 &lt;= x)</code> becomes <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>, which is always <code>&lt;= 20</code>!"},{"h":"<code>x &gt;= 10 &amp;&amp; x &lt;= 20</code>","ok":1,"why":"Both comparisons must be combined with <code>&amp;&amp;</code>."},{"h":"<code>x &gt;= 10 || x &lt;= 20</code>","why":"Every number is either <code>&gt;= 10</code> or <code>&lt;= 20</code>!"},{"h":"<code>10 &lt;= x &amp; x &lt;= 20</code>","why":"<code>&amp;</code> is bitwise AND; logical AND is <code>&amp;&amp;</code>."}],"why":"Always split chained inequalities using <code>&amp;&amp;</code>."}]],"id":"c-07","title":"Operators and expressions","goal":"use arithmetic, assignment, increment/decrement, relational and logical operators and predict operator precedence","week":"6","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"integer division","m":"division of two integers that truncates any remainder","tr":"tam sayı bölmesi"},{"t":"modulo (%)","m":"returns the remainder of integer division","tr":"mod (kalan) operatörü"},{"t":"type casting","m":"converting a value to another type, e.g. `(double)a / b`","tr":"tür dönüşümü"},{"t":"short-circuit evaluation","m":"skipping the right side of `&&` or `||` once the result is already known","tr":"kısa devre değerlendirme"}]},"c-08":{"html":"<p>Until now, every line in <code>main</code> ran from top to bottom. <strong>Decision structures</strong> let your program choose different paths based on conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-ifelse\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-if-else-if-and-else\"><h2 id=\"if-else-if-and-else\"><code>if</code>, <code>else if</code> and <code>else</code></h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\" data-stdin=\"78\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int score;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &amp;score);\n\n    if (score &gt;= 90) {\n        printf(\"Grade: A\\n\");\n    } else if (score &gt;= 80) {\n        printf(\"Grade: B\\n\");\n    } else if (score &gt;= 50) {\n        printf(\"Grade: C\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Grade: F\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<p>In an <code>if ... else if ... else</code> chain, C checks conditions from top to bottom and runs <strong>only the first</strong> matching branch.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-ternary-operator\"><h2 id=\"the-ternary-operator\">The ternary operator <code>?:</code></h2>\n<p>A compact one-line <code>if-else</code> for picking between two values: <code>condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false</code></p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>int max = (a &gt; b) ? a : b;\nprintf(\"%s\\n\", (n % 2 == 0) ? \"even\" : \"odd\");</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-switch-case\"><h2 id=\"switch-case\"><code>switch-case</code></h2>\n<p>When you compare <strong>one integer or <code>char</code> expression</strong> against exact constant values, <code>switch</code> is cleaner than many <code>else if</code>s.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\" data-stdin=\"2\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int day;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &amp;day);\n\n    switch (day) {\n        case 1:\n            printf(\"Monday\\n\");\n            break;\n        case 2:\n            printf(\"Tuesday\\n\");\n            break;\n        case 3:\n            printf(\"Wednesday\\n\");\n            break;\n        default:\n            printf(\"Other day\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Always remember <code>break;</code> in <code>switch</code>!</div><p>If you forget <code>break;</code>, execution <strong>falls through</strong> into the next <code>case</code> below it — even if that case's value doesn't match! Exam bug-hunt questions test this all the time.</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Practice <code>if</code>, <code>else if</code>, <code>?:</code> and <code>switch</code> in the exercises below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-08\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting a semicolon right after <code>if (x &gt; 5);</code> — the <code>;</code> acts as an empty body, so the <code>{ ... }</code> block runs unconditionally!</li><li>Forgetting <code>break;</code> at the end of a <code>case</code> in <code>switch</code>, causing unintended fall-through.</li><li>Using <code>switch</code> with a <code>float</code>/<code>double</code> or a string (<code>\"yes\"</code>) — <code>switch</code> only works on integer types (<code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>enum</code>).</li><li>Writing <code>if (x = 5)</code> instead of <code>if (x == 5)</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>branching</strong></td><td>choosing between different code paths using a condition</td><td lang=\"tr\">dallanma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ternary operator</strong></td><td>compact <code>cond ? a : b</code> conditional expression</td><td lang=\"tr\">üçlü operatör</td></tr><tr><td><strong>switch-case</strong></td><td>multi-way branch matching an integer or char against constant cases</td><td lang=\"tr\">switch-case yapısı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>fall-through</strong></td><td>continuing into the next <code>case</code> when <code>break</code> is omitted</td><td lang=\"tr\">alta geçme (fall-through)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does this print when <code>x = 2</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>B</code>","why":"There is no <code>break;</code> after <code>case 2</code>, so it falls through into <code>case 3</code>."},{"h":"<code>BC</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>case 2</code> prints <code>B</code>, falls through to <code>case 3</code> which prints <code>C</code>, then <code>break;</code> exits."},{"h":"<code>BCD</code>","why":"<code>case 3</code> has a <code>break;</code>, so it stops before <code>default</code>."},{"h":"<code>D</code>","why":"<code>x</code> matches <code>case 2</code>."}],"why":"Without <code>break;</code>, <code>switch</code> falls through to subsequent cases until it hits a <code>break;</code> or <code>}</code>.","code":{"lang":"c","src":"switch (x) {\n    case 1: printf(\"A\"); break;\n    case 2: printf(\"B\");\n    case 3: printf(\"C\"); break;\n    default: printf(\"D\");\n}"}},{"q":"What does <code>if (x &gt; 10); printf(\"Big\\n\");</code> print when <code>x = 3</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Nothing","why":"The <code>;</code> ends the <code>if</code> immediately!"},{"h":"<code>Big</code>","ok":1,"why":"The semicolon makes the <code>if</code> body empty; <code>printf(\"Big\\n\");</code> always runs."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"An empty statement <code>;</code> is legal C, just almost always a bug."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"It prints <code>Big</code>."}],"why":"Never put a semicolon <code>;</code> right after <code>if (...)</code>."},{"q":"Which type is <strong>NOT</strong> allowed in <code>switch (expr)</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>int</code>","why":"Allowed."},{"h":"<code>char</code>","why":"Allowed (<code>char</code> is a small integer)."},{"h":"<code>double</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>switch</code> only accepts integer/character types, not floating-point or strings."},{"h":"<code>long</code>","why":"Allowed."}],"why":"<code>switch</code> works on <code>int</code> and <code>char</code>, not <code>float</code>, <code>double</code>, or strings."}]],"id":"c-08","title":"Decision structures: if, else and switch","goal":"branch program execution using if, else if, nested if, the ternary operator ?: and switch-case","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"branching","m":"choosing between different code paths using a condition","tr":"dallanma"},{"t":"ternary operator","m":"compact `cond ? a : b` conditional expression","tr":"üçlü operatör"},{"t":"switch-case","m":"multi-way branch matching an integer or char against constant cases","tr":"switch-case yapısı"},{"t":"fall-through","m":"continuing into the next `case` when `break` is omitted","tr":"alta geçme (fall-through)"}]},"c-09":{"html":"<p>A <strong>loop</strong> repeats a block of code as long as a condition stays true. C has three loop statements:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Loop</th><th>When to use it</th><th>Key rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>for</code></td><td>You know how many times to repeat (e.g. <code>1 .. N</code>)</td><td><code>for (init; cond; step)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>while</code></td><td>You repeat until a condition changes (e.g. reading until <code>0</code>)</td><td>Checks condition <strong>before</strong> each pass (may run 0 times)</td></tr><tr><td><code>do-while</code></td><td>Menu or input validation that must run <strong>at least once</strong></td><td>Checks condition <strong>after</strong> each pass; ends with <code>;</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-while-for-and-do-while-in-action\"><h2 id=\"while-for-and-do-while-in-action\"><code>while</code>, <code>for</code> and <code>do-while</code> in action</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    // 1. for loop: 1 to 5\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n\n    // 2. while loop: count down\n    int c = 3;\n    while (c &gt; 0) {\n        printf(\"%d \", c);\n        c--;\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n\n    // 3. do-while: runs at least once even if condition is false\n    int x = 10;\n    do {\n        printf(\"x=%d\\n\", x);\n    } while (x &lt; 5);\n\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-break-continue-and-nested-loops\"><h2 id=\"break-continue-and-nested-loops\"><code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> and nested loops</h2>\n<ul><li><strong><code>break;</code>:</strong> immediately exits the innermost loop.</li><li><strong><code>continue;</code>:</strong> skips the rest of the current pass and jumps to the next iteration (in <code>for</code>, it still runs <code>step</code>!).</li><li><strong>Nested loops:</strong> a loop inside a loop — for each pass of the outer loop, the inner loop runs all the way through.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 6; i++) {\n        if (i == 3) continue;  // skip 3\n        if (i == 5) break;     // stop at 5\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the loop exercises below!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-09\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting a semicolon after <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++);</code> or <code>while (x &gt; 0);</code> — that creates an empty loop (and with <code>while</code>, an <strong>infinite loop</strong>!).</li><li>Off-by-one errors: writing <code>i &lt; 10</code> when you wanted <code>1..10</code> (<code>i &lt;= 10</code>).</li><li>Forgetting the semicolon at the end of <code>do { ... } while (cond);</code>.</li><li>Forgetting to update the loop variable in <code>while</code>, causing an infinite loop.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>iteration</strong></td><td>one single pass through the body of a loop</td><td lang=\"tr\">yineleme (iterasyon)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>infinite loop</strong></td><td>a loop whose condition never becomes false</td><td lang=\"tr\">sonsuz döngü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>break</strong></td><td>immediately exits the enclosing loop or switch</td><td lang=\"tr\">döngüyü kırma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>continue</strong></td><td>skips the rest of the current iteration and starts the next</td><td lang=\"tr\">sonraki adıma atlama</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"How many times does <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; i++)</code> run?","opts":[{"h":"<code>5</code> times","why":"It runs for <code>0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</code>."},{"h":"<code>6</code> times","ok":1,"why":"<code>0</code> through <code>5</code> inclusive is 6 iterations."},{"h":"<code>4</code> times","why":"<code>&lt;= 5</code> includes <code>5</code>."},{"h":"<code>0</code> times","why":"<code>0 &lt;= 5</code> is true."}],"why":"<code>0..5</code> inclusive is <code>5 - 0 + 1 = 6</code> iterations."},{"q":"What is guaranteed about a <code>do { ... } while (cond);</code> loop?","opts":[{"h":"It runs forever","why":"It stops when <code>cond</code> is false."},{"h":"Its body always executes <strong>at least once</strong>","ok":1,"why":"The condition is checked at the bottom, after the body runs."},{"h":"It never checks <code>cond</code>","why":"It checks <code>cond</code> after each pass."},{"h":"It runs faster than <code>for</code>","why":"Speed is identical."}],"why":"<code>do-while</code> is post-test, so the body always runs at least once."},{"q":"What does this print? <code>for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) { if (i % 2 == 0) continue; printf(\"%d\", i); }</code>","opts":[{"h":"<code>24</code>","why":"Even numbers trigger <code>continue</code>, which skips <code>printf</code>."},{"h":"<code>135</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>1, 3, 5</code> are printed; <code>2, 4</code> are skipped."},{"h":"<code>12345</code>","why":"<code>continue</code> skips <code>printf</code> when <code>i</code> is even."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"<code>continue</code> skips to the next iteration; it does not exit the loop like <code>break</code>."}],"why":"<code>continue</code> skips the rest of the loop body for that pass and moves to <code>i++</code>."}]],"id":"c-09","title":"Loops: while, for and do-while","goal":"repeat actions with while, for and do-while loops and control them with break and continue","week":"9","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"iteration","m":"one single pass through the body of a loop","tr":"yineleme (iterasyon)"},{"t":"infinite loop","m":"a loop whose condition never becomes false","tr":"sonsuz döngü"},{"t":"break","m":"immediately exits the enclosing loop or switch","tr":"döngüyü kırma"},{"t":"continue","m":"skips the rest of the current iteration and starts the next","tr":"sonraki adıma atlama"}]},"c-10":{"html":"<p>Week 10 puts <code>if</code>, <code>switch</code> and loops together on the classic problems every C course tests in labs and written exams.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-1-star-patterns-nested-loops\"><h2 id=\"1-star-patterns-nested-loops\">1. Star patterns (nested loops)</h2>\n<p>The <strong>outer</strong> loop controls the <strong>rows</strong>; the <strong>inner</strong> loop controls <strong>how many <code>*</code>s</strong> on that row.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int n = 4;\n    for (int row = 1; row &lt;= n; row++) {\n        for (int col = 1; col &lt;= row; col++) {\n            printf(\"*\");\n        }\n        printf(\"\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-digit-problems-10-and-10\"><h2 id=\"2-digit-problems-10-and-10\">2. Digit problems (<code>% 10</code> and <code>/ 10</code>)</h2>\n<p>To peel off digits of an integer <code>n</code> from right to left:</p>\n<ul><li><code>n % 10</code> gives the <strong>last digit</strong>.</li><li><code>n /= 10</code> removes the <strong>last digit</strong>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\" data-stdin=\"4729\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int n, sum = 0, rev = 0;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &amp;n);\n    while (n &gt; 0) {\n        int d = n % 10;\n        sum += d;\n        rev = rev * 10 + d;\n        n /= 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"Digit sum = %d, Reverse = %d\\n\", sum, rev);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-prime-check-fibonacci-and-gcd\"><h2 id=\"3-prime-check-fibonacci-and-gcd\">3. Prime check, Fibonacci and GCD</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Prime check:</strong> A number <code>n &gt;= 2</code> is prime if no integer <code>d</code> from <code>2</code> to <code>n - 1</code> (or <code>d * d &lt;= n</code>) divides <code>n</code> evenly (<code>n % d == 0</code>).</li><li><strong>Fibonacci:</strong> <code>0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, ...</code> — each term is the sum of the previous two (<code>next = a + b; a = b; b = next;</code>).</li><li><strong>GCD (Euclidean algorithm):</strong> Repeat <code>r = a % b; a = b; b = r;</code> until <code>b == 0</code>; then <code>a</code> is the GCD!</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-menu-driven-program-switch-inside-do-while\"><h2 id=\"4-menu-driven-program-switch-inside-do-while\">4. Menu-driven program (<code>switch</code> inside <code>do-while</code>)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\" data-stdin=\"1 2 0\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int choice;\n    do {\n        scanf(\"%d\", &amp;choice);\n        switch (choice) {\n            case 1: printf(\"Hello!\\n\"); break;\n            case 2: printf(\"2 + 2 = 4\\n\"); break;\n            case 0: printf(\"Bye!\\n\"); break;\n            default: printf(\"Invalid\\n\");\n        }\n    } while (choice != 0);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the pattern and number exercises below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-10\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>In prime checking, printing <code>\"Prime\"</code> inside the <code>for</code> loop on the first non-divisor instead of waiting until the loop finishes (use a <code>int isPrime = 1;</code> flag and set <code>isPrime = 0; break;</code> if a divisor is found).</li><li>Forgetting <code>printf(\"\\n\");</code> at the end of each outer-loop row in a star pattern.</li><li>Treating <code>1</code> as a prime number (<code>1</code> is <strong>not</strong> prime; primes start at <code>2</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>nested loop</strong></td><td>a loop placed inside the body of another loop</td><td lang=\"tr\">iç içe döngü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>flag variable</strong></td><td>an <code>int</code> (<code>0</code> or <code>1</code>) tracking whether a condition was met</td><td lang=\"tr\">bayrak değişkeni</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Euclidean algorithm</strong></td><td>fast repeated-remainder method to find the GCD of two numbers</td><td lang=\"tr\">Öklid algoritması</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"How do you get the <strong>last digit</strong> of a positive integer <code>n</code> in C?","opts":[{"h":"<code>n / 10</code>","why":"<code>n / 10</code> removes the last digit and keeps the rest."},{"h":"<code>n % 10</code>","ok":1,"why":"Remainder upon dividing by 10 is the ones digit."},{"h":"<code>n * 10</code>","why":"Shifts digits left."},{"h":"<code>n - 10</code>","why":"Just subtracts 10."}],"why":"Use <code>n % 10</code> to read the last digit and <code>n /= 10</code> to drop it."},{"q":"In a prime-number checker for <code>n</code>, why is <code>1</code> NOT prime?","opts":[{"h":"Because <code>1</code> is odd","why":"<code>3, 5, 7</code> are odd and prime."},{"h":"By definition, a prime number has <strong>exactly two</strong> positive divisors (<code>1</code> and itself), so <code>n</code> must be <code>&gt;= 2</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>1</code> has only one divisor."},{"h":"Because <code>1 % 2 == 1</code>","why":"Oddness doesn't disqualify primes."},{"h":"Because C cannot divide by 1","why":"C divides by 1 fine."}],"why":"Always check <code>if (n &lt; 2) isPrime = 0;</code> first!"},{"q":"How many <code>*</code> characters does this print in total?","opts":[{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"That would be a single loop."},{"h":"<code>9</code>","why":"<code>3 * 3</code> is for a square (<code>j &lt;= 3</code>)."},{"h":"<code>6</code>","ok":1,"why":"Row 1 prints 1, row 2 prints 2, row 3 prints 3: <code>1 + 2 + 3 = 6</code>."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","why":"Count <code>1 + 2 + 3</code>."}],"why":"Triangular nested loop prints <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"1 + 2 + 3 = 6\"></span> stars.","code":{"lang":"c","src":"for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)\n    for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++)\n        printf(\"*\");"}}]],"id":"c-10","title":"Decision and loop practice: patterns and number problems","goal":"write nested-loop star patterns, number algorithms (prime, digit sum, reverse, Fibonacci, GCD) and switch-in-loop menus","week":"10","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"nested loop","m":"a loop placed inside the body of another loop","tr":"iç içe döngü"},{"t":"flag variable","m":"an `int` (`0` or `1`) tracking whether a condition was met","tr":"bayrak değişkeni"},{"t":"Euclidean algorithm","m":"fast repeated-remainder method to find the GCD of two numbers","tr":"Öklid algoritması"}]},"c-11":{"html":"<p>Putting all your code inside <code>main</code> quickly becomes messy. A <strong>function</strong> (<em>fonksiyon</em>) is a reusable named block of code that does one specific job.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-function\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-prototype-definition-and-call\"><h2 id=\"prototype-definition-and-call\">Prototype, definition and call</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Prototype (declaration)</strong> at the top tells the compiler the function's name, return type and parameter types.</li><li><strong>Definition</strong> has the <code>{ ... }</code> body.</li><li><strong>Call</strong> runs the function with actual arguments.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint add(int a, int b);          // 1. prototype\nvoid greet(void);               // void = returns nothing\n\nint main() {\n    greet();\n    int total = add(12, 8);     // 3. call\n    printf(\"Sum = %d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nvoid greet(void) {              // 2. definition\n    printf(\"Welcome to C functions!\\n\");\n}\n\nint add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-scope-and-pass-by-value\"><h2 id=\"scope-and-pass-by-value\">Scope and pass-by-value</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Local variable:</strong> declared inside <code>{ ... }</code> of a function; exists only while that function runs.</li><li><strong>Global variable:</strong> declared outside all functions; visible everywhere (use sparingly!).</li><li><strong>Pass-by-value:</strong> C passes a <strong>copy</strong> of each argument to the function. Changing a parameter inside the function does <strong>not</strong> change the caller's variable!</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid tryChange(int x) {\n    x = 999;   // changes only the local copy!\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int num = 10;\n    tryChange(num);\n    printf(\"num is still %d\\n\", num);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-recursion-intro-fact-n\"><h2 id=\"recursion-intro-fact-n\">Recursion intro (<code>fact(n)</code>)</h2>\n<p>A <strong>recursive function</strong> calls itself on a smaller input until it hits a <strong>base case</strong> (stopping condition).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint fact(int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;        // base case\n    return n * fact(n - 1);      // recursive step\n}\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"5! = %d\\n\", fact(5));\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the function and recursion exercises below!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-11\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting <code>return</code> in a non-<code>void</code> function (e.g. <code>int max(int a, int b)</code>).</li><li>Putting a semicolon <code>;</code> after the header of a function <strong>definition</strong>: <code>int add(int a, int b); { ... }</code> — only <strong>prototypes</strong> end with <code>;</code>.</li><li>Writing a recursive function without a <strong>base case</strong>, causing stack overflow / infinite recursion.</li><li>Expecting <code>void swap(int a, int b)</code> without pointers to swap variables in <code>main</code> (pass-by-value only swaps local copies!).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>function prototype</strong></td><td>header ending in <code>;</code> telling the compiler a function's signature</td><td lang=\"tr\">fonksiyon prototipi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>return type</strong></td><td>type of the value sent back by <code>return</code> (<code>void</code> if none)</td><td lang=\"tr\">dönüş tipi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>scope</strong></td><td>region of code where a variable is visible (local vs global)</td><td lang=\"tr\">kapsam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>pass-by-value</strong></td><td>passing a copy of a variable's value into a function</td><td lang=\"tr\">değer ile aktarım</td></tr><tr><td><strong>recursion</strong></td><td>a function calling itself with a smaller problem and a base case</td><td lang=\"tr\">özyineleme (rekürsif)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does <code>void</code> mean as a function's return type?","opts":[{"h":"The function returns <code>0</code>","why":"<code>int</code> functions return <code>0</code>, <code>void</code> functions return no value."},{"h":"The function does not return any value","ok":1,"why":"Use <code>return;</code> (optional) with no expression."},{"h":"The function takes no parameters","why":"<code>void</code> in the <em>parameter</em> list means no parameters; as return type it means no return value."},{"h":"The function cannot call <code>printf</code>","why":"<code>void</code> functions can run any statements, including <code>printf</code>."}],"why":"<code>void</code> as return type means the function returns nothing."},{"q":"What does this print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>15</code>","why":"C uses pass-by-value, so <code>bump</code> only modifies its own copy <code>n</code>."},{"h":"<code>5</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>a</code> in <code>main</code> is unchanged."},{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"<code>a</code> stays <code>5</code>."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"Valid C."}],"why":"Because C passes arguments by value, changing <code>n</code> inside <code>bump</code> does not affect <code>a</code>.","code":{"lang":"c","src":"void bump(int n) { n += 10; }\nint main() { int a = 5; bump(a); printf(\"%d\\n\", a); return 0; }"}},{"q":"What happens if a recursive function has no base case?","opts":[{"h":"It returns <code>0</code> automatically","why":"C never inserts a base case."},{"h":"It keeps calling itself until memory (the call stack) runs out","ok":1,"why":"Always write a stopping condition first."},{"h":"It runs only once","why":"Without a base case, it never stops calling itself."},{"h":"The compiler converts it to a <code>for</code> loop","why":"It runs recursively until stack overflow."}],"why":"Every recursive function needs a base case that stops the recursion."}]],"id":"c-11","title":"Functions and recursion","goal":"declare, define and call functions, understand scope and pass-by-value, and write simple recursive functions","week":"11","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"function prototype","m":"header ending in `;` telling the compiler a function's signature","tr":"fonksiyon prototipi"},{"t":"return type","m":"type of the value sent back by `return` (`void` if none)","tr":"dönüş tipi"},{"t":"scope","m":"region of code where a variable is visible (local vs global)","tr":"kapsam"},{"t":"pass-by-value","m":"passing a copy of a variable's value into a function","tr":"değer ile aktarım"},{"t":"recursion","m":"a function calling itself with a smaller problem and a base case","tr":"özyineleme (rekürsif)"}]},"c-12":{"html":"<p>When you need to store 50 student grades, creating <code>g1, g2, ..., g50</code> is impossible to manage. An <strong>array</strong> (<em>dizi</em>) stores a fixed-size list of values of the <strong>same type</strong> side by side in memory.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-array\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-1d-arrays-and-0-based-indexing\"><h2 id=\"1d-arrays-and-0-based-indexing\">1D arrays and 0-based indexing</h2>\n<p>An array of size <code>N</code> has valid indices <strong><code>0</code> through <code>N - 1</code></strong>!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int grades[5] = {80, 95, 70, 88, 60};\n    int sum = 0, max = grades[0];\n\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {\n        sum += grades[i];\n        if (grades[i] &gt; max) max = grades[i];\n    }\n    printf(\"Sum = %d, Avg = %.1f, Max = %d\\n\", sum, sum / 5.0, max);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-linear-search-and-bubble-sort\"><h2 id=\"linear-search-and-bubble-sort\">Linear search and bubble sort</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Linear search:</strong> Check <code>arr[0], arr[1], ...</code> one by one until <code>arr[i] == target</code> (return index <code>i</code>, or <code>-1</code> if not found).</li><li><strong>Bubble sort:</strong> Repeatedly compare adjacent pairs <code>arr[j]</code> and <code>arr[j + 1]</code> and swap them if out of order.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid sort(int a[], int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n - 1 - i; j++) {\n            if (a[j] &gt; a[j + 1]) {\n                int tmp = a[j];\n                a[j] = a[j + 1];\n                a[j + 1] = tmp;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5] = {40, 10, 50, 20, 30};\n    sort(a, 5);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<p>Notice something important: when you pass an array <code>a</code> to a function <code>sort(a, 5)</code>, C passes the <strong>starting address</strong> of the array — so modifying <code>a[j]</code> inside the function <strong>does</strong> modify the original array in <code>main</code>! Also, always pass the length <code>n</code> as a separate parameter.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2d-arrays-tables-matrices\"><h2 id=\"2d-arrays-tables-matrices\">2D arrays (tables / matrices)</h2>\n<p>A 2D array is a grid of rows and columns: <code>int m[2][3] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6} };</code>. Use two nested loops: outer for rows, inner for columns.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the array exercises below!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-12\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Out-of-bounds access:</strong> writing <code>arr[5]</code> on <code>int arr[5]</code> — valid indices are <code>0..4</code>, so <code>arr[5]</code> reads/corrupts neighbouring memory!</li><li>Comparing or copying two arrays with <code>a == b</code> or <code>a = b</code> — you must loop element by element.</li><li>Initializing <code>max = 0</code> instead of <code>max = arr[0]</code> (fails when all array elements are negative).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>array</strong></td><td>a contiguous fixed-size collection of elements of the same type</td><td lang=\"tr\">dizi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>index</strong></td><td>zero-based position of an element (<code>0</code> to <code>size - 1</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">indis (indeks)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>linear search</strong></td><td>scanning an array from index 0 until the target is found</td><td lang=\"tr\">doğrusal arama</td></tr><tr><td><strong>bubble sort</strong></td><td>sorting by repeatedly swapping out-of-order adjacent elements</td><td lang=\"tr\">kabarcık sıralaması</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"For <code>int a[8];</code>, what is the index of the <strong>last</strong> element?","opts":[{"h":"<code>8</code>","why":"<code>a[8]</code> is out of bounds!"},{"h":"<code>7</code>","ok":1,"why":"Indices run from <code>0</code> to <code>size - 1</code>, so <code>0 .. 7</code>."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"C arrays are 0-indexed, not 1-indexed."},{"h":"<code>9</code>","why":"Out of bounds."}],"why":"An array of size <code>N</code> has indices <code>0</code> through <code>N - 1</code>."},{"q":"If you pass <code>int nums[5]</code> to <code>void doubleAll(int a[], int n)</code> and multiply each <code>a[i]</code> by 2, what happens to <code>nums</code> in <code>main</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>nums</code> stays unchanged","why":"Arrays are NOT copied by value; the function receives the array's address."},{"h":"<code>nums</code> in <code>main</code> IS doubled","ok":1,"why":"Changes to <code>a[i]</code> modify the caller's array directly."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"Passing <code>int a[]</code> is standard C."},{"h":"Only <code>nums[0]</code> changes","why":"The loop updates all <code>n</code> elements."}],"why":"Passing an array passes a pointer to its first element, so the function modifies the original array."},{"q":"In <code>int grid[3][4];</code>, how many <code>int</code> elements does <code>grid</code> hold?","opts":[{"h":"<code>7</code>","why":"Multiply rows × columns, don't add."},{"h":"<code>12</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>3</code> rows × <code>4</code> columns = <code>12</code> elements."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"<code>3</code> is only the number of rows."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","why":"<code>4</code> is only the number of columns."}],"why":"A 2D array <code>T[rows][cols]</code> holds <code>rows * cols</code> elements."}]],"id":"c-12","title":"Arrays (1D, searching, sorting and 2D)","goal":"declare and loop over 1D and 2D arrays, search and sort them, and pass arrays to functions","week":"12","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"array","m":"a contiguous fixed-size collection of elements of the same type","tr":"dizi"},{"t":"index","m":"zero-based position of an element (`0` to `size - 1`)","tr":"indis (indeks)"},{"t":"linear search","m":"scanning an array from index 0 until the target is found","tr":"doğrusal arama"},{"t":"bubble sort","m":"sorting by repeatedly swapping out-of-order adjacent elements","tr":"kabarcık sıralaması"}]},"c-13":{"html":"<p>Every variable you declare lives at a numbered byte location in RAM, called its <strong>memory address</strong>. A <strong>pointer</strong> (<em>işaretçi</em>) is simply a variable whose value is the <strong>memory address</strong> of another variable.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-pointer\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-address-of-and-dereference\"><h2 id=\"address-of-and-dereference\"><code>&amp;</code> (address-of) and <code>*</code> (dereference)</h2>\n<p>Two operators do all the work:</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>&amp;x</code>:</strong> gives the <strong>address</strong> of <code>x</code> (where <code>x</code> lives in RAM).</li><li><strong><code>int *p = &amp;x;</code>:</strong> declares <code>p</code> as a pointer-to-<code>int</code>, storing <code>x</code>'s address.</li><li><strong><code>*p</code>:</strong> (<strong>dereference</strong>) follows the address stored in <code>p</code> to read or change <code>x</code> itself!</li></ul>\n<p><em>(To print a pointer address safely in <code>printf</code>, cast to <code>(void *)p</code> and use <code>%p</code>.)</em></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-watch-it-in-memory-step-by-step\"><h2 id=\"watch-it-in-memory-step-by-step\">Watch it in memory step by step</h2>\n<p>Press <strong>Next ▶</strong> in the memory visualizer below and watch <code>p</code> store <code>x</code>'s address, and <code>*p = 99</code> change <code>x</code>!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"memviz\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    int *p = &amp;x;\n    *p = 99;\n    printf(\"x = %d, *p = %d\\n\", x, *p);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-null-pointers\"><h2 id=\"null-pointers\"><code>NULL</code> pointers</h2>\n<p>If a pointer does not point to any valid variable yet, initialize it to <strong><code>NULL</code></strong>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>int *p = NULL;\nif (p != NULL) {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Never dereference (<code>*p</code>) a <code>NULL</code> or uninitialized pointer — it crashes the program!</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the pointer basics exercises below.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-13\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing <code>*</code> in a <strong>declaration</strong> (<code>int *p;</code> = <code>p</code> is a pointer) with <code>*</code> in an <strong>expression</strong> (<code>*p = 5;</code> = go to the address in <code>p</code> and set the value to <code>5</code>).</li><li>Declaring <code>int* a, b;</code> thinking both are pointers — in C, <code>a</code> is <code>int *</code>, while <code>b</code> is a plain <code>int</code>! Write <code>int *a, *b;</code>.</li><li>Dereferencing an uninitialized or <code>NULL</code> pointer (<code>int *p; *p = 5;</code> → crash!).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>memory address</strong></td><td>the numbered location of a variable in RAM</td><td lang=\"tr\">bellek adresi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>pointer</strong></td><td>a variable that stores a memory address</td><td lang=\"tr\">işaretçi (pointer)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>dereference (*)</strong></td><td>accessing the value at the address stored in a pointer</td><td lang=\"tr\">adresteki değere erişme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NULL</strong></td><td>a special pointer value meaning \"points to nothing\"</td><td lang=\"tr\">NULL (boş işaretçi)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does <code>*p = 25;</code> do when <code>int x = 10; int *p = &amp;x;</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Changes the address stored in <code>p</code> to <code>25</code>","why":"<code>*p</code> accesses the <em>target variable</em> (<code>x</code>), not the address inside <code>p</code>."},{"h":"Changes the value of <code>x</code> to <code>25</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>p</code> points to <code>x</code>, so <code>*p = 25</code> modifies <code>x</code>."},{"h":"Creates a new variable <code>25</code>","why":"No new variable is created."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"Valid pointer dereference."}],"why":"<code>*p</code> means \"follow <code>p</code> to the variable it points to\" — here, <code>x</code>."},{"q":"What is <code>int *a, b;</code> in C?","opts":[{"h":"Both <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are pointers to <code>int</code>","why":"The <code>*</code> attaches only to <code>a</code>."},{"h":"<code>a</code> is a pointer to <code>int</code>, and <code>b</code> is a regular <code>int</code>","ok":1,"why":"Write <code>int *a, *b;</code> if you want two pointers."},{"h":"Both <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are regular <code>int</code>s","why":"<code>a</code> has <code>*</code>, so <code>a</code> is a pointer."},{"h":"Syntax error","why":"Valid C, though easy to misread."}],"why":"In <code>int *a, b;</code>, <code>*</code> belongs only to <code>a</code>."},{"q":"Why should you initialize an unused pointer to <code>NULL</code>?","opts":[{"h":"So <code>*p</code> prints <code>0</code>","why":"Dereferencing <code>NULL</code> still crashes!"},{"h":"So you can check <code>if (p != NULL)</code> before dereferencing, instead of holding a random garbage address","ok":1,"why":"<code>NULL</code> is a safe \"no target\" sentinel."},{"h":"To double its size","why":"Pointers are always pointer-sized (8 bytes on 64-bit)."},{"h":"So <code>scanf</code> can read into it without <code>&amp;</code>","why":"Unrelated."}],"why":"Always initialize unused pointers to <code>NULL</code> and check <code>p != NULL</code> before <code>*p</code>."}]],"id":"c-13","title":"Pointers (Part 1): Addresses, & and *","goal":"understand memory addresses, declare pointers, dereference them with *, and use NULL safely","week":"13","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"memory address","m":"the numbered location of a variable in RAM","tr":"bellek adresi"},{"t":"pointer","m":"a variable that stores a memory address","tr":"işaretçi (pointer)"},{"t":"dereference (*)","m":"accessing the value at the address stored in a pointer","tr":"adresteki değere erişme"},{"t":"NULL","m":"a special pointer value meaning \"points to nothing\"","tr":"NULL (boş işaretçi)"}]},"c-14":{"html":"<p>Now that you know <code>&amp;</code> and <code>*</code>, let's see the two biggest reasons C uses pointers: <strong>changing caller variables from a function</strong> and <strong>working with arrays</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-pass-by-reference-the-swap-function\"><h2 id=\"pass-by-reference-the-swap-function\">Pass-by-reference: the <code>swap</code> function</h2>\n<p>Remember from Lesson 11 that <code>void badSwap(int a, int b)</code> only swaps local copies. By passing <strong>addresses</strong> (<code>&amp;x, &amp;y</code>) into pointer parameters (<code>int *a, int *b</code>), the function reaches back into <code>main</code>'s variables!</p>\n<p>Step through this in the memory visualizer to see <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> pointing into <code>main</code>'s stack frame:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"memviz\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) {\n    int tmp = *a;\n    *a = *b;\n    *b = tmp;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 3, y = 7;\n    swap(&amp;x, &amp;y);\n    printf(\"x=%d y=%d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-pointers-and-arrays\"><h2 id=\"pointers-and-arrays\">Pointers and arrays</h2>\n<p>In C, an array name <code>arr</code> (when used in an expression) decays to the <strong>address of its first element</strong> (<code>&amp;arr[0]</code>).</p>\n<ul><li><code>int *p = arr;</code> points to <code>arr[0]</code>.</li><li><code>p + 1</code> does <strong>not</strong> add 1 byte — it advances by <code>1 * sizeof(int)</code> (4 bytes) to point to <code>arr[1]</code>!</li><li>In general, <code>*(p + i)</code> is the exact same element as <code>arr[i]</code>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    int *p = arr;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n        printf(\"arr[%d] = %d, *(p+%d) = %d\\n\", i, arr[i], i, *(p + i));\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the pointer-function and array-pointer exercises below!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-14\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing <code>swap(x, y)</code> instead of <code>swap(&amp;x, &amp;y)</code> when <code>swap</code> expects <code>int *</code> pointers.</li><li>Writing <code>*p++</code> expecting to increment the <em>value</em> <code>*p</code> — <code>++</code> binds tighter than <code>*</code>, so <code>*p++</code> reads <code>*p</code> and then moves <code>p</code> to the next element! Use <code>(*p)++</code> to increment the value.</li><li>Incrementing a pointer past the end of the array and dereferencing it.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>pass-by-reference</strong></td><td>passing variable addresses (<code>&amp;x</code>) so a function can modify the caller's variables</td><td lang=\"tr\">adres ile aktarım</td></tr><tr><td><strong>pointer arithmetic</strong></td><td>adding <code>k</code> to a pointer moves by <code>k * sizeof(*p)</code> bytes</td><td lang=\"tr\">işaretçi aritmetiği</td></tr><tr><td><strong>array decay</strong></td><td>an array name turning into a pointer to its first element (<code>&amp;arr[0]</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">dizi indirgenmesi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Given <code>int a[4] = {5, 10, 15, 20}; int *p = a;</code>, what is <code>*(p + 2)</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>5</code>","why":"<code>*(p + 0)</code> is <code>5</code>."},{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"<code>*(p + 1)</code> is <code>10</code>."},{"h":"<code>15</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>p + 2</code> points to <code>a[2]</code>, which is <code>15</code>."},{"h":"<code>7</code>","why":"Pointer arithmetic steps by whole <code>int</code>s, not adding <code>2</code> to <code>5</code>."}],"why":"<code>*(p + i)</code> is identical to <code>a[i]</code>."},{"q":"How must you call <code>void reset(int *n) { *n = 0; }</code> for <code>int score = 50;</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>reset(score);</code>","why":"Passes an <code>int</code>, not an <code>int *</code> address."},{"h":"<code>reset(&amp;score);</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>&amp;score</code> passes the address of <code>score</code>."},{"h":"<code>reset(*score);</code>","why":"<code>score</code> is not a pointer."},{"h":"<code>reset(&amp;int score);</code>","why":"Invalid syntax."}],"why":"Pass <code>&amp;score</code> when a function parameter is <code>int *</code>."},{"q":"If <code>p</code> is an <code>int *</code> (<code>sizeof(int) == 4</code>) pointing to address <code>0x1000</code>, what address does <code>p + 3</code> point to?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0x1003</code>","why":"Pointer arithmetic scales by <code>sizeof(int)</code>, not 1 byte."},{"h":"<code>0x100C</code> (<code>0x1000 + 3 * 4 = 0x1000 + 12</code>)","ok":1,"why":"Adding <code>3</code> to an <code>int *</code> advances by <code>3 * 4 = 12</code> bytes (<code>0xC</code>)."},{"h":"<code>0x1006</code>","why":"<code>3 * 2 = 6</code> would be for a 2-byte <code>short</code>."},{"h":"<code>0x1030</code>","why":"<code>3 * 16 = 48</code>."}],"why":"Adding <code>k</code> to <code>T *p</code> advances the address by <code>k * sizeof(T)</code> bytes."}]],"id":"c-14","title":"Pointers (Part 2): Arrays and pass-by-reference","goal":"use pointers to modify caller variables (swap) and walk through arrays using pointer arithmetic","week":"13","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"pass-by-reference","m":"passing variable addresses (`&x`) so a function can modify the caller's variables","tr":"adres ile aktarım"},{"t":"pointer arithmetic","m":"adding `k` to a pointer moves by `k * sizeof(*p)` bytes","tr":"işaretçi aritmetiği"},{"t":"array decay","m":"an array name turning into a pointer to its first element (`&arr[0]`)","tr":"dizi indirgenmesi"}]},"c-15":{"html":"<p>C does not have a built-in <code>string</code> type like Python or Java. Instead, a <strong>string</strong> in C is an <strong>array of <code>char</code> ending with the null character <code>'\\0'</code></strong> (ASCII code <code>0</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-strings\"></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-char-ascii-and-the-0-terminator\"><h2 id=\"char-ascii-and-the-0-terminator\"><code>char</code>, ASCII and the <code>'\\0'</code> terminator</h2>\n<p>Every <code>char</code> is stored as a small integer ASCII code: <code>'A'</code> = <code>65</code>, <code>'a'</code> = <code>97</code>, <code>'0'</code> = <code>48</code>. If you store <code>\"Cat\"</code> in <code>char s[10]</code>, memory holds: <code>'C'</code>, <code>'a'</code>, <code>'t'</code>, <code>'\\0'</code>. Functions stop reading when they hit <code>'\\0'</code>, so <strong>always leave 1 extra byte for <code>'\\0'</code></strong>!</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-string-h-and-ctype-h\"><h2 id=\"string-h-and-ctype-h\"><code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;ctype.h&gt;</code></h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Library</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>strlen(s)</code></td><td><code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code></td><td>Returns length <strong>excluding</strong> <code>'\\0'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>strcpy(dst, src)</code></td><td><code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code></td><td>Copies <code>src</code> into <code>dst</code> (make sure <code>dst</code> is big enough!)</td></tr><tr><td><code>strcat(dst, src)</code></td><td><code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code></td><td>Appends <code>src</code> onto the end of <code>dst</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>strcmp(a, b)</code></td><td><code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code></td><td>Returns <strong><code>0</code></strong> if <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are equal (<code>&lt;0</code> if <code>a&lt;b</code>, <code>&gt;0</code> if <code>a&gt;b</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>toupper(c)</code> / <code>tolower(c)</code></td><td><code>&lt;ctype.h&gt;</code></td><td>Converts a letter to upper/lower case</td></tr><tr><td><code>isdigit(c)</code> / <code>isalpha(c)</code></td><td><code>&lt;ctype.h&gt;</code></td><td>Checks if <code>c</code> is <code>'0'..'9'</code> or a letter</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    char word[30] = \"Hello\";\n    strcat(word, \" C\");\n    printf(\"word = %s (len = %d)\\n\", word, (int)strlen(word));\n\n    if (strcmp(word, \"Hello C\") == 0) {\n        printf(\"Exact match!\\n\");\n    }\n\n    for (int i = 0; word[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        word[i] = toupper(word[i]);\n    }\n    printf(\"Upper: %s\\n\", word);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-strings-scanf-s-s-vs-fgets\"><h2 id=\"reading-strings-scanf-s-s-vs-fgets\">Reading strings: <code>scanf(\"%s\", s)</code> vs <code>fgets</code></h2>\n<ul><li><code>scanf(\"%s\", name);</code> (no <code>&amp;</code> needed because <code>name</code> is an array!) stops at the <strong>first space</strong>.</li><li><code>fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin);</code> reads a <strong>whole line including spaces</strong>, up to <code>sizeof(line) - 1</code> chars (and usually keeps the trailing <code>'\\n'</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the character and string exercises below!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-15\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Comparing strings with <code>if (s1 == s2)</code> — that compares <strong>memory addresses</strong>, not text! Always use <code>if (strcmp(s1, s2) == 0)</code>.</li><li>Writing <code>if (strcmp(s1, s2))</code> thinking <code>1</code> means equal — <code>strcmp</code> returns <strong><code>0</code></strong> when equal!</li><li>Assigning a string after declaration with <code>s = \"Ali\";</code> — use <code>strcpy(s, \"Ali\");</code>.</li><li>Forgetting space for <code>'\\0'</code>: <code>\"Hello\"</code> needs at least <code>char s[6]</code>, not <code>char s[5]</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>null terminator ('\\0')</strong></td><td>the zero byte marking the end of a C string</td><td lang=\"tr\">sonlandırma karakteri ('\\0')</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ASCII</strong></td><td>standard numeric encoding for characters (<code>'A'=65</code>, <code>'a'=97</code>, <code>'0'=48</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">ASCII kodu</td></tr><tr><td><strong>strcmp</strong></td><td>compares two strings; returns <code>0</code> when they are identical</td><td lang=\"tr\">metin karşılaştırma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>fgets</strong></td><td>safely reads a full line of text (with spaces) into a <code>char</code> array</td><td lang=\"tr\">satır okuma</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does <code>strcmp(\"cat\", \"cat\")</code> return?","opts":[{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"<code>1</code> (or positive) means the first string is greater, not equal!"},{"h":"<code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>strcmp</code> returns <code>0</code> when two strings are identical."},{"h":"<code>-1</code>","why":"Negative means the first string comes before the second."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"<code>strlen</code> returns <code>3</code>, not <code>strcmp</code>."}],"why":"Always write <code>if (strcmp(a, b) == 0)</code> to check if two C strings are equal."},{"q":"How many bytes does <code>char s[] = \"IGU\";</code> allocate in memory?","opts":[{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"Don't forget the hidden <code>'\\0'</code> at the end!"},{"h":"<code>4</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>'I'</code>, <code>'G'</code>, <code>'U'</code>, and <code>'\\0'</code> = 4 bytes."},{"h":"<code>2</code>","why":"Too small."},{"h":"<code>5</code>","why":"Only one <code>'\\0'</code> is added."}],"why":"A string literal of length <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"k\"></span> takes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"k + 1\"></span> bytes (including <code>'\\0'</code>)."},{"q":"Which function reads a full line <strong>with spaces</strong> safely without overflowing <code>char buf[50]</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>scanf(\"%s\", buf);</code>","why":"Stops at the first space and doesn't limit length."},{"h":"<code>fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin);</code>","ok":1,"why":"Reads spaces and stops before overflowing <code>buf</code>."},{"h":"<code>scanf(\"%c\", &amp;buf);</code>","why":"Reads only 1 character."},{"h":"<code>strcpy(buf, stdin);</code>","why":"<code>stdin</code> is a <code>FILE *</code>, not a string."}],"why":"Use <code>fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin)</code> to read a whole line safely."}]],"id":"c-15","title":"Characters and strings","goal":"work with ASCII characters, null-terminated strings, <string.h> functions, <ctype.h> functions, and fgets","week":"14","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"null terminator ('\\0')","m":"the zero byte marking the end of a C string","tr":"sonlandırma karakteri ('\\0')"},{"t":"ASCII","m":"standard numeric encoding for characters (`'A'=65`, `'a'=97`, `'0'=48`)","tr":"ASCII kodu"},{"t":"strcmp","m":"compares two strings; returns `0` when they are identical","tr":"metin karşılaştırma"},{"t":"fgets","m":"safely reads a full line of text (with spaces) into a `char` array","tr":"satır okuma"}]},"c-16":{"html":"<p>When printing reports or grade tables, messy unaligned columns are hard to read. <code>printf</code> gives you <strong>width</strong> and <strong>precision</strong> controls right inside <code>%...</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-width-alignment-and-precision\"><h2 id=\"width-alignment-and-precision\">Width, alignment and precision</h2>\n<p>Syntax: <code>%[flags][width][.precision]specifier</code></p>\n<ul><li><code>%5d</code>: print <code>int</code> in a column at least <strong>5 characters wide</strong>, right-aligned (<code>   42</code>).</li><li><code>%-10s</code>: <code>-</code> means <strong>left-aligned</strong> in a 10-char column (<code>Deniz     </code>).</li><li><code>%05d</code>: <code>0</code> pads with leading zeros (<code>00042</code>).</li><li><code>%8.2f</code>: <strong>8</strong> characters wide total, <strong>2</strong> digits after the decimal point (<code>   85.50</code>).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%-10s %5s %8s\\n\", \"Name\", \"ID\", \"GPA\");\n    printf(\"%-10s %05d %8.2f\\n\", \"Deniz\", 42, 3.456);\n    printf(\"%-10s %05d %8.2f\\n\", \"Ece\", 107, 3.8);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-return-values-of-printf-and-scanf\"><h2 id=\"return-values-of-printf-and-scanf\">Return values of <code>printf</code> and <code>scanf</code></h2>\n<ul><li><code>printf(...)</code> returns the <strong>number of characters printed</strong> (e.g. <code>printf(\"Hi\\n\")</code> prints <code>Hi</code> + newline and returns <code>3</code>).</li><li><code>scanf(...)</code> returns the <strong>number of items successfully read</strong> (e.g. <code>while (scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) == 1)</code> keeps reading until input ends or a non-number is typed).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve the formatted I/O exercises below!</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"exercises\" data-lesson=\"c-16\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking <code>%6.2f</code> means \"6 digits before the dot and 2 after\" — <code>6</code> is the <strong>total</strong> minimum width (including digits, the <code>.</code>, and minus sign).</li><li>Confusing <code>%-10s</code> (left-aligned) with <code>%10s</code> (right-aligned).</li><li>Expecting <code>%5d</code> to chop off a 7-digit number like <code>1234567</code> — width is a <em>minimum</em>, so C prints all 7 digits without truncating.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>field width</strong></td><td>minimum number of characters a printed value occupies</td><td lang=\"tr\">alan genişliği</td></tr><tr><td><strong>precision</strong></td><td>number of decimal digits for <code>%f</code> (or max chars for <code>%s</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">hassasiyet (duyarlılık)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>left-aligned</strong></td><td>padding spaces on the right using <code>-</code> (e.g. <code>%-10s</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">sola hizalı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"[%-5d]\", 42);</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>[   42]</code>","why":"Without <code>-</code>, it right-aligns."},{"h":"<code>[42   ]</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>-5d</code> left-aligns <code>42</code> in a 5-character field, padding 3 spaces on the right."},{"h":"<code>[00042]</code>","why":"Zero-padding uses <code>%05d</code>."},{"h":"<code>[42]</code>","why":"Minimum width 5 adds 3 spaces."}],"why":"<code>-</code> inside <code>%...</code> means left-align."},{"q":"What does <code>int n = printf(\"AB\\n\");</code> store in <code>n</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>2</code>","why":"<code>\\n</code> also counts as 1 printed character."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>'A'</code>, <code>'B'</code>, and <code>'\\n'</code> = 3 characters printed."},{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"<code>0</code> is <code>main</code>'s success return, not <code>printf</code>'s."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","why":"The null terminator is not printed."}],"why":"<code>printf</code> returns the total count of characters it printed."},{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"%6.1f\", 3.46);</code> print (use <code>·</code> for spaces)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>···3.4</code>","why":"<code>printf</code> rounds <code>3.46</code> to <code>3.5</code> for <code>.1f</code>."},{"h":"<code>···3.5</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>\"3.5\"</code> is 3 characters, so 3 leading spaces pad it to width 6."},{"h":"<code>3.5000</code>","why":"<code>.1f</code> prints 1 digit after the decimal point."},{"h":"<code>····3.5</code>","why":"That would be 7 characters wide."}],"why":"<code>%6.1f</code> rounds to 1 decimal place (<code>3.5</code>, 3 chars) and pads with 3 leading spaces to reach width 6."}]],"id":"c-16","title":"Formatted input and output","goal":"align tables with printf field width and precision, use printf's return value, and handle scanf formats","week":"15","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"field width","m":"minimum number of characters a printed value occupies","tr":"alan genişliği"},{"t":"precision","m":"number of decimal digits for `%f` (or max chars for `%s`)","tr":"hassasiyet (duyarlılık)"},{"t":"left-aligned","m":"padding spaces on the right using `-` (e.g. `%-10s`)","tr":"sola hizalı"}]}},"bank":[{"q":"Which component holds running programs and variables temporarily, and loses its contents when power is turned off?","opts":[{"h":"CPU","why":"The CPU executes instructions; it is not main working memory."},{"h":"RAM","ok":1,"why":"RAM is fast, volatile working memory."},{"h":"SSD","why":"SSD is permanent (non-volatile) storage."},{"h":"GPU","why":"GPU handles graphics/parallel compute, not main system memory."}],"why":"RAM is volatile workspace cleared on power-off; permanent files live on SSD/HDD.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which statement about <strong>C</strong> is true?","opts":[{"h":"C is an interpreted language like Python","why":"C is compiled before running."},{"h":"C uses a compiler (such as <code>gcc</code>) to translate source code into machine code before execution","ok":1,"why":"Compiled executables run directly on the CPU."},{"h":"C requires a web browser to run","why":"Standard C compiles to native machine code."},{"h":"C cannot work with hardware or memory","why":"C is famous for low-level memory access."}],"why":"C is a compiled language: <code>.c</code> source → compiler (<code>gcc</code>) → machine code executable.","tag":"01"},{"q":"What is the correct order of steps to run a C program from scratch?","opts":[{"h":"Run → Compile → Write source","why":"You cannot run what hasn't been written or compiled."},{"h":"Write source code → Compile → Run executable","ok":1,"why":"Source → machine code → execution."},{"h":"Compile → Write source → Link","why":"You must write the <code>.c</code> file first."},{"h":"Write machine code → Compile to C","why":"Compilers translate high-level C <em>into</em> machine code."}],"why":"Write <code>main.c</code> → compile with <code>gcc main.c -o main</code> → run <code>./main</code>.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which part of the computer performs arithmetic (<code>+</code>, <code>-</code>) and logic comparisons?","opts":[{"h":"CPU","ok":1,"why":"The Central Processing Unit (specifically its ALU) executes arithmetic and logic."},{"h":"RAM","why":"RAM stores data and instructions."},{"h":"Hard drive","why":"Storage holds files long-term."},{"h":"Keyboard","why":"Input device."}],"why":"The CPU is the processor that fetches and executes instructions.","tag":"01"},{"q":"How do the <strong>Internet</strong> and the <strong>World Wide Web</strong> relate?","opts":[{"h":"They are the exact same thing","why":"The Web is just one service on the Internet."},{"h":"The Internet is the global network of connected networks; the Web is the system of hypertext pages (HTTP/HTML) running on top of it","ok":1,"why":"Email and SSH also use the Internet without being the Web."},{"h":"The Web existed before ARPANET","why":"ARPANET (1969) came 20 years before the Web (1989)."},{"h":"The Internet runs inside a single web browser","why":"The Internet is global infrastructure."}],"why":"ARPANET (late 1960s) + TCP/IP → the Internet; Tim Berners-Lee (1989–91) → the Web.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which property MUST every valid algorithm have?","opts":[{"h":"It must be written in C","why":"Algorithms are language-independent."},{"h":"It must consist of clear, ordered steps and finish in a finite number of steps","ok":1,"why":"Infinite or vague steps are not valid algorithms."},{"h":"It must take at least 3 inputs","why":"Some algorithms take 0, 1, or many inputs."},{"h":"It must contain a <code>switch</code> statement","why":"<code>switch</code> is a C construct, not an algorithm requirement."}],"why":"An algorithm is finite, unambiguous, and ordered.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Why do programmers write <strong>pseudocode</strong> before coding?","opts":[{"h":"Because <code>gcc</code> compiles pseudocode faster","why":"<code>gcc</code> cannot compile pseudocode."},{"h":"To design and check the logic in plain human-readable steps without syntax distractions","ok":1,"why":"Once the logic is clear, translating to C is easy."},{"h":"To encrypt the program","why":"Pseudocode is not encryption."},{"h":"To measure CPU clock speed","why":"Unrelated."}],"why":"Pseudocode helps you focus on logic before syntax.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Trace: <code>x = 2; y = 3; x = x + y; y = x - y; x = x - y;</code>. What are <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> at the end?","opts":[{"h":"<code>x = 2, y = 3</code>","why":"The steps swap the values."},{"h":"<code>x = 3, y = 2</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>x=5</code>, <code>y=5-3=2</code>, <code>x=5-2=3</code> — a classic no-temp swap!"},{"h":"<code>x = 5, y = 2</code>","why":"The last step sets <code>x = 5 - 2 = 3</code>."},{"h":"<code>x = 5, y = 5</code>","why":"Check the subtractions carefully."}],"why":"Tracing line by line: <code>(2,3) → (5,3) → (5,2) → (3,2)</code>.","tag":"02"},{"q":"In pseudocode, what should happen if a student's <code>score</code> is checked with <code>IF score &gt;= 50 THEN OUTPUT \"Pass\" ELSE OUTPUT \"Fail\"</code> for <code>score = 50</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Output <code>\"Fail\"</code>","why":"<code>&gt;=</code> includes <code>50</code>."},{"h":"Output <code>\"Pass\"</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>50 &gt;= 50</code> is true."},{"h":"Nothing is printed","why":"<code>ELSE</code> handles false; here the condition is true."},{"h":"Error","why":"Valid comparison."}],"why":"<code>&gt;=</code> means greater than or equal to, so <code>50</code> passes.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which step is missing in: <code>START; READ n; sum = sum + n; OUTPUT sum; STOP</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>END</code>","why":"<code>STOP</code> is already there."},{"h":"Initializing <code>sum = 0</code> before adding <code>n</code>","ok":1,"why":"Without <code>sum = 0</code>, <code>sum</code>'s starting value is undefined."},{"h":"Declaring <code>int main()</code>","why":"Not needed in pseudocode."},{"h":"Dividing by 2","why":"Not part of summing."}],"why":"Always initialize accumulators like <code>sum = 0</code> before using them.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which flowchart symbol represents a <strong>Yes/No decision</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Oval / rounded pill","why":"Marks Start and End."},{"h":"Rectangle","why":"Represents a process/calculation."},{"h":"Diamond","ok":1,"why":"Represents a condition with two outgoing arrows (Yes/No)."},{"h":"Parallelogram","why":"Represents Input/Output."}],"why":"Diamond = Decision; Parallelogram = I/O; Rectangle = Process; Oval = Start/End.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which flowchart symbol should contain <code>Read a, b</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Rectangle","why":"Rectangles are for calculations/assignments."},{"h":"Parallelogram","ok":1,"why":"Input and Output use a parallelogram."},{"h":"Diamond","why":"Diamonds are for conditions."},{"h":"Circle","why":"Standard flowcharts use ovals for Start/End."}],"why":"Input/Output operations use a parallelogram.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which flowchart symbol should contain <code>avg = (a + b) / 2.0</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Rectangle","ok":1,"why":"Calculations and assignments go in a Process rectangle."},{"h":"Parallelogram","why":"Parallelograms are for <code>Read</code> and <code>Print</code>."},{"h":"Diamond","why":"Not a Yes/No question."},{"h":"Oval","why":"Only for Start and End."}],"why":"Assignments and math formulas belong in Process rectangles.","tag":"03"},{"q":"What is wrong with a flowchart diamond that has only <strong>one</strong> arrow leaving it?","opts":[{"h":"Nothing, that is normal","why":"A decision must branch!"},{"h":"A decision tests a true/false condition and must have <strong>two</strong> outgoing branches (<code>Yes</code> and <code>No</code>)","ok":1,"why":"Otherwise it cannot branch."},{"h":"Diamonds should have zero outgoing arrows","why":"Only <code>End</code> has zero outgoing arrows."},{"h":"Diamonds cannot have incoming arrows","why":"Every step after Start has at least one incoming arrow."}],"why":"Every Decision diamond needs both a Yes/True branch and a No/False branch.","tag":"03"},{"q":"How is a <strong>loop</strong> shown in a flowchart?","opts":[{"h":"With a bigger rectangle","why":"Size doesn't make a loop."},{"h":"With a flow arrow pointing <strong>back up</strong> to an earlier decision/step","ok":1,"why":"Repeating until the condition becomes false."},{"h":"With two <code>Start</code> ovals","why":"A flowchart has only one <code>Start</code>."},{"h":"Loops cannot be drawn in flowcharts","why":"Flowcharts represent loops with back-arrows."}],"why":"A flow line returning to an earlier node forms a loop.","tag":"03"},{"q":"In a flowchart to find the <strong>maximum</strong> of <code>N</code> input numbers, what should <code>max</code> start as?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"Fails if all inputs are negative!"},{"h":"The <strong>first</strong> number read","ok":1,"why":"Guaranteed to work for negative and positive numbers alike."},{"h":"<code>N</code>","why":"<code>N</code> is the count, not a value."},{"h":"<code>1000</code>","why":"Fails if all inputs are smaller than 1000!"}],"why":"Always initialize <code>max</code> (and <code>min</code>) to the first value read.","tag":"04"},{"q":"In a factorial (<code>N!</code>) flowchart, what must <code>fact</code> be initialized to?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"Multiplying by <code>0</code> always gives <code>0</code>."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>1</code> is the multiplicative identity (and <code>0! = 1</code>)."},{"h":"<code>N</code>","why":"Would compute <code>N * N!</code>."},{"h":"<code>-1</code>","why":"Would flip the sign."}],"why":"Initialize product accumulators to <code>1</code> and sum accumulators to <code>0</code>.","tag":"04"},{"q":"In the <code>sum = 1 + 2 + ... + N</code> flowchart (<code>sum = 0, i = 1</code>, while <code>i &lt;= N</code>: <code>sum = sum + i; i = i + 1</code>), what happens if <code>N = 0</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Infinite loop","why":"<code>i &lt;= N</code> (<code>1 &lt;= 0</code>) is immediately false, so the loop doesn't run."},{"h":"It prints <code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"Loop is skipped and <code>sum</code> remains <code>0</code>."},{"h":"It prints <code>1</code>","why":"<code>i</code> is not added when <code>1 &lt;= 0</code> is false."},{"h":"It crashes","why":"Valid comparison."}],"why":"Because <code>i &lt;= N</code> is checked before the body, <code>N = 0</code> prints <code>0</code>.","tag":"04"},{"q":"When finding the <strong>largest of 3 numbers (<code>a, b, c</code>)</strong>, which approach is simplest and least error-prone?","opts":[{"h":"Checking all 6 permutations","why":"Too many branches."},{"h":"Set <code>max = a</code>, then <code>if (b &gt; max) max = b</code>, then <code>if (c &gt; max) max = c</code>","ok":1,"why":"Clean, linear, and easy to extend to <code>N</code> numbers."},{"h":"Add <code>a + b + c</code>","why":"Gives the sum, not the max."},{"h":"Sort only <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>","why":"Ignores <code>c</code>."}],"why":"Starting with <code>max = a</code> and updating with each remaining value needs only 2 comparisons.","tag":"04"},{"q":"In a grade-letter flowchart (<code>&gt;= 90</code> A, <code>&gt;= 70</code> B, <code>&gt;= 50</code> C, else F), what does <code>score = 85</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>A</code>","why":"<code>85 &gt;= 90</code> is false."},{"h":"<code>B</code>","ok":1,"why":"First check <code>&gt;= 90</code> is false; second check <code>85 &gt;= 70</code> is true → <code>B</code>."},{"h":"<code>C</code>","why":"It already took the <code>B</code> branch and jumped to End."},{"h":"<code>BC</code>","why":"In an <code>else-if</code> chain, only one branch runs."}],"why":"Each <code>No</code> branch leads to the next threshold, and each <code>Yes</code> branch prints and jumps to <code>End</code>.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Which <code>#include</code> header is required for <code>printf</code> and <code>scanf</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>#include &lt;string.h&gt;</code>","why":"For string functions like <code>strlen</code>, <code>strcpy</code>."},{"h":"<code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Standard Input/Output header."},{"h":"<code>#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;</code>","why":"For character checks like <code>isdigit</code>, <code>toupper</code>."},{"h":"<code>#include &lt;math.h&gt;</code>","why":"For math functions like <code>sqrt</code>, <code>pow</code>."}],"why":"<code>&lt;stdio.h&gt;</code> provides <code>printf</code>, <code>scanf</code>, <code>fgets</code>, <code>fopen</code>, etc.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Which of these is an <strong>invalid</strong> C variable name?","opts":[{"h":"<code>_total</code>","why":"Leading underscore is valid (though avoid double underscores)."},{"h":"<code>gpa2</code>","why":"Digits after the first character are valid."},{"h":"<code>2nd_grade</code>","ok":1,"why":"Variable names cannot start with a digit."},{"h":"<code>maxValue</code>","why":"Valid camelCase identifier."}],"why":"Identifiers must start with a letter or <code>_</code>, never a digit.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Which C type stores <code>'Z'</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>int</code>","why":"<code>int</code> is for whole numbers."},{"h":"<code>double</code>","why":"<code>double</code> is for decimals."},{"h":"<code>char</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>char</code> stores a single character in 1 byte."},{"h":"<code>float</code>","why":"<code>float</code> is for decimals."}],"why":"Use <code>char</code> with single quotes <code>'Z'</code>.","tag":"05"},{"q":"What is the value of <code>x</code> right after <code>int x;</code> (with no <code>= ...</code>) inside <code>main()</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Guaranteed to be <code>0</code>","why":"C does NOT zero-initialize local variables!"},{"h":"Uninitialized (indeterminate garbage)","ok":1,"why":"Reading it before assigning causes undefined behavior."},{"h":"<code>NULL</code>","why":"<code>NULL</code> is for pointers."},{"h":"<code>-1</code>","why":"Not guaranteed."}],"why":"Always initialize local variables before reading them.","tag":"05"},{"q":"What does <code>sizeof(char)</code> always equal in C?","opts":[{"h":"<code>1</code> byte","ok":1,"why":"By the C standard, <code>sizeof(char)</code> is always <code>1</code>."},{"h":"<code>2</code> bytes","why":"That's <code>short</code> (typically) or Java's <code>char</code>, not C's <code>char</code>."},{"h":"<code>4</code> bytes","why":"<code>4</code> is typical for <code>int</code> and <code>float</code>."},{"h":"<code>8</code> bytes","why":"<code>8</code> is typical for <code>double</code> and 64-bit pointers."}],"why":"<code>sizeof(char)</code> is always 1 byte in C.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Which <code>printf</code> format specifier prints a <code>double</code> rounded to 2 decimal places?","opts":[{"h":"<code>%d</code>","why":"<code>%d</code> is for <code>int</code>."},{"h":"<code>%.2f</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>.2f</code> prints 2 digits after the decimal point."},{"h":"<code>%2f</code>","why":"<code>2</code> before the dot is minimum width, not precision."},{"h":"<code>%c</code>","why":"<code>%c</code> prints a <code>char</code>."}],"why":"<code>%.2f</code> formats <code>float</code> or <code>double</code> to 2 decimal places.","tag":"06"},{"q":"What is wrong with <code>double d; scanf(\"%f\", &amp;d);</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Missing <code>;</code>","why":"Semicolons are fine."},{"h":"<code>%f</code> in <code>scanf</code> expects a <code>float*</code>, not a <code>double*</code>; you must use <code>%lf</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>printf</code> accepts <code>%f</code> for both, but <code>scanf</code> requires <code>%lf</code> for <code>double</code>."},{"h":"<code>&amp;d</code> should be <code>d</code>","why":"<code>scanf</code> requires <code>&amp;d</code>."},{"h":"<code>double</code> cannot be read with <code>scanf</code>","why":"It can, with <code>%lf</code>."}],"why":"In <code>scanf</code>, <code>%f</code> = <code>float</code>, <code>%lf</code> = <code>double</code>.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Why do we often write <code>scanf(\" %c\", &amp;ch);</code> (with a leading space) after reading an <code>int</code>?","opts":[{"h":"To print a space","why":"<code>scanf</code> reads, it doesn't print."},{"h":"To skip any leftover whitespace or newline (<code>\\n</code>) from the previous <code>Enter</code> key","ok":1,"why":"Without the space, <code>%c</code> reads the <code>\\n</code>!"},{"h":"To convert <code>ch</code> to uppercase","why":"<code>toupper</code> does that."},{"h":"It is a syntax error","why":"Leading space in <code>scanf</code> format is valid and skips whitespace."}],"why":"A space in <code>\" %c\"</code> consumes leftover <code>\\n</code> and spaces before reading the character.","tag":"06"},{"q":"How do you print a literal <code>%</code> sign using <code>printf</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>\\%</code>","why":"Backslash does not escape <code>%</code>."},{"h":"<code>%%</code>","ok":1,"why":"Double percent prints one <code>%</code>."},{"h":"<code>%</code>","why":"A single <code>%</code> starts a format specifier."},{"h":"<code>\"%\"</code> alone","why":"Inside <code>printf(\"%\")</code>, <code>%</code> expects a specifier letter."}],"why":"Use <code>%%</code> inside <code>printf</code> to output a single <code>%</code>.","tag":"06"},{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"A\\nB\\tC\");</code> output?","opts":[{"h":"<code>A\\nB\\tC</code>","why":"<code>\\n</code> and <code>\\t</code> are escape sequences."},{"h":"<code>A</code> on line 1, then <code>B</code>, a tab, and <code>C</code> on line 2","ok":1,"why":"<code>\\n</code> = newline, <code>\\t</code> = tab."},{"h":"<code>ABC</code> on one line","why":"<code>\\n</code> breaks the line."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"Valid escape sequences."}],"why":"<code>\\n</code> inserts a newline; <code>\\t</code> inserts a horizontal tab.","tag":"06"},{"q":"What is the value of <code>9 / 4</code> in C?","opts":[{"h":"<code>2.25</code>","why":"Both <code>9</code> and <code>4</code> are <code>int</code>, so integer division truncates."},{"h":"<code>2</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>int / int</code> drops the <code>.25</code> remainder."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"Integer division never rounds up."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"<code>9 % 4</code> is <code>1</code>; <code>9 / 4</code> is <code>2</code>."}],"why":"Integer division truncates toward zero: <code>9 / 4 == 2</code>, <code>9 % 4 == 1</code>.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What does <code>int x = 5; int y = ++x;</code> produce?","opts":[{"h":"<code>x = 5, y = 5</code>","why":"<code>++x</code> increments <code>x</code>."},{"h":"<code>x = 6, y = 5</code>","why":"That would be postfix <code>y = x++</code>."},{"h":"<code>x = 6, y = 6</code>","ok":1,"why":"Prefix <code>++x</code> increments <code>x</code> first, then returns the new value <code>6</code>."},{"h":"<code>x = 5, y = 6</code>","why":"<code>x</code> is also <code>6</code>."}],"why":"Prefix <code>++x</code>: increment first, then use; Postfix <code>x++</code>: use first, then increment.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What is <code>2 + 3 * 4 % 5</code> in C?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"<code>*</code> and <code>%</code> have higher precedence than <code>+</code>, and associate left-to-right."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>3 * 4 = 12</code>, <code>12 % 5 = 2</code>, <code>2 + 2 = 4</code>."},{"h":"<code>20</code>","why":"<code>(2 + 3) * 4 = 20</code>, ignoring <code>% 5</code>."},{"h":"<code>14</code>","why":"Missing <code>% 5</code>."}],"why":"<code>*</code> and <code>%</code> run left-to-right before <code>+</code>: <code>2 + ((3 * 4) % 5) = 2 + (12 % 5) = 2 + 2 = 4</code>.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What is the value of <code>5 &gt; 3 &amp;&amp; 2 &gt; 10</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"<code>2 &gt; 10</code> is false, so <code>&amp;&amp;</code> returns <code>0</code>."},{"h":"<code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>&amp;&amp;</code> is true (<code>1</code>) only if <em>both</em> sides are true."},{"h":"<code>5</code>","why":"Logical operators return <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>."},{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"Logical operators return <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>."}],"why":"<code>1 &amp;&amp; 0</code> evaluates to <code>0</code> (false).","tag":"07"},{"q":"What happens in <code>int a = 1, b = 0; if (a || (b = 7)) {}</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>b</code> becomes <code>7</code>","why":"Because <code>a</code> is already true (<code>1</code>), <code>||</code> short-circuits and skips <code>(b = 7)</code>!"},{"h":"<code>b</code> stays <code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"Short-circuit evaluation skips the right side of <code>||</code> when the left side is true."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"Valid C."},{"h":"<code>a</code> becomes <code>0</code>","why":"<code>a</code> is not modified."}],"why":"<code>||</code> short-circuits when the left side is true; <code>&amp;&amp;</code> short-circuits when the left side is false.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What does <code>if (x = 0) printf(\"Yes\"); else printf(\"No\");</code> do?","opts":[{"h":"Prints <code>Yes</code>","why":"<code>x = 0</code> assigns <code>0</code> to <code>x</code>, and <code>0</code> is false!"},{"h":"Sets <code>x</code> to <code>0</code> and prints <code>No</code>","ok":1,"why":"Assignment returns the assigned value (<code>0</code>), which is false."},{"h":"Fails to compile","why":"C allows assignment inside <code>if</code>, though it's usually a bug!"},{"h":"Leaves <code>x</code> unchanged","why":"<code>=</code> overwrites <code>x</code>."}],"why":"Always use <code>==</code> for comparison; <code>=</code> is assignment.","tag":"08"},{"q":"What does <code>(8 &gt; 5) ? 100 : 200</code> evaluate to?","opts":[{"h":"<code>100</code>","ok":1,"why":"Because <code>8 &gt; 5</code> is true, the ternary operator returns the first value (<code>100</code>)."},{"h":"<code>200</code>","why":"<code>200</code> is returned only when the condition is false."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"It returns <code>100</code>, not <code>1</code>."},{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"It returns <code>100</code>."}],"why":"<code>cond ? a : b</code> evaluates to <code>a</code> if <code>cond</code> is true, else <code>b</code>.","tag":"08"},{"q":"In a <code>switch (k)</code> block, when does <code>default:</code> run?","opts":[{"h":"Always at the end","why":"Only if no <code>case</code> matched (or if a matching <code>case</code> above it lacked <code>break;</code>)."},{"h":"When <code>k</code> does not match any <code>case</code> (or falls through into it)","ok":1,"why":"<code>default</code> is the fallback branch."},{"h":"Only when <code>k == 0</code>","why":"<code>case 0:</code> matches <code>0</code>."},{"h":"Before <code>case 1:</code>","why":"It runs only if no case matches."}],"why":"<code>default:</code> handles any value not matched by the <code>case</code> labels.","tag":"08"},{"q":"What does <code>int x = 1; switch (x) { case 1: printf(\"A\"); case 2: printf(\"B\"); break; }</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>A</code>","why":"Missing <code>break;</code> after <code>case 1</code>!"},{"h":"<code>AB</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>case 1</code> prints <code>A</code>, falls through to <code>case 2</code> printing <code>B</code>, then <code>break;</code> stops."},{"h":"<code>B</code>","why":"<code>x</code> is <code>1</code>, so <code>case 1</code> runs first."},{"h":"Nothing","why":"<code>case 1</code> matches."}],"why":"Always put <code>break;</code> at the end of each <code>case</code> unless fall-through is intentional.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Which condition checks if <code>n</code> is divisible by <strong>both</strong> 3 and 5?","opts":[{"h":"<code>n % 3 == 0 || n % 5 == 0</code>","why":"<code>||</code> means \"either 3 or 5\"."},{"h":"<code>n % 3 == 0 &amp;&amp; n % 5 == 0</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>&amp;&amp;</code> requires both remainders to be <code>0</code>."},{"h":"<code>n / 3 == 0 &amp;&amp; n / 5 == 0</code>","why":"<code>/</code> is quotient, not remainder!"},{"h":"<code>n % 3 &amp;&amp; n % 5</code>","why":"Non-zero remainder is true in C, so that checks <em>not</em> divisible!"}],"why":"Divisible by <code>k</code> means <code>n % k == 0</code>.","tag":"08"},{"q":"How many times does <code>for (int i = 2; i &lt; 10; i += 2)</code> run?","opts":[{"h":"<code>5</code>","why":"Stops before <code>10</code>: <code>2, 4, 6, 8</code> is 4 times."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","ok":1,"why":"Runs for <code>i = 2, 4, 6, 8</code>."},{"h":"<code>8</code>","why":"Step is <code>+= 2</code>, not <code>++</code>."},{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"Starts at 2 and steps by 2."}],"why":"Trace the values of <code>i</code>: <code>2, 4, 6, 8</code> → 4 iterations.","tag":"09"},{"q":"What is the difference between <code>break</code> and <code>continue</code> inside a loop?","opts":[{"h":"Both exit the loop immediately","why":"Only <code>break</code> exits the loop."},{"h":"<code>break</code> exits the loop completely; <code>continue</code> skips the rest of the current pass and jumps to the next iteration","ok":1,"why":"In <code>for</code>, <code>continue</code> still runs the step (<code>i++</code>)."},{"h":"<code>continue</code> restarts <code>main()</code>","why":"It only advances to the next loop iteration."},{"h":"<code>break</code> only works in <code>switch</code>","why":"<code>break</code> works in <code>switch</code> and all loops (<code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>do-while</code>)."}],"why":"<code>break</code> = stop looping; <code>continue</code> = skip this pass, keep looping.","tag":"09"},{"q":"What happens when you write <code>int i = 1; while (i &lt;= 5); { printf(\"%d\", i); i++; }</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Prints <code>12345</code>","why":"The <code>;</code> after <code>while (...)</code> makes the loop body empty!"},{"h":"Infinite loop (prints nothing)","ok":1,"why":"<code>i</code> stays <code>1</code>, so <code>1 &lt;= 5</code> stays true forever."},{"h":"Prints <code>1</code>","why":"Never reaches <code>{ ... }</code>."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"Syntactically valid, logically an infinite loop."}],"why":"Never put a semicolon <code>;</code> right after <code>while (...)</code> or <code>for (...)</code>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which loop is best when you want to ask a user for a password <strong>at least once</strong> and repeat while it is wrong?","opts":[{"h":"<code>for</code>","why":"<code>for</code> checks before the first run."},{"h":"<code>while</code>","why":"<code>while</code> checks before the first run."},{"h":"<code>do-while</code>","ok":1,"why":"Post-test loop guaranteed to run at least once."},{"h":"<code>switch</code>","why":"<code>switch</code> is a branch, not a loop."}],"why":"Use <code>do { ... } while (...);</code> when the body must execute at least once.","tag":"09"},{"q":"How many stars does <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) for (int j = 0; j &lt; 4; j++) printf(\"*\");</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>7</code>","why":"Nested loops multiply: <code>3 * 4</code>, not <code>3 + 4</code>."},{"h":"<code>12</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>3</code> outer iterations × <code>4</code> inner iterations = <code>12</code>."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"Inner loop runs 4 times per outer pass."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","why":"Outer loop runs 3 times."}],"why":"Nested loops multiply iteration counts: <code>3 × 4 = 12</code>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"How do you remove the last decimal digit from a positive integer <code>n</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>n = n % 10;</code>","why":"<code>n % 10</code> <em>keeps</em> only the last digit."},{"h":"<code>n = n / 10;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Integer division by 10 drops the ones digit (e.g. <code>472 / 10 == 47</code>)."},{"h":"<code>n = n - 10;</code>","why":"Subtracts 10 instead of shifting digits."},{"h":"<code>n = n * 10;</code>","why":"Adds a trailing zero."}],"why":"<code>n % 10</code> = last digit; <code>n / 10</code> = remaining digits.","tag":"10"},{"q":"In Euclid's GCD algorithm (<code>while (b != 0) { r = a % b; a = b; b = r; }</code>), what holds the GCD when the loop stops?","opts":[{"h":"<code>b</code>","why":"<code>b</code> is <code>0</code> when the loop stops."},{"h":"<code>a</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>a</code> holds the last non-zero divisor, which is the GCD."},{"h":"<code>r</code>","why":"<code>r</code> is <code>0</code> on the final step."},{"h":"<code>a * b</code>","why":"<code>0</code>."}],"why":"When <code>b</code> reaches <code>0</code>, <code>a</code> is the GCD.","tag":"10"},{"q":"What are the first 6 Fibonacci numbers starting from <code>0, 1</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0, 1, 1, 2, 4, 8</code>","why":"<code>2 + 1 = 3</code>, not <code>4</code>."},{"h":"<code>0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5</code>","ok":1,"why":"Each number is the sum of the previous two."},{"h":"<code>1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6</code>","why":"That's counting numbers."},{"h":"<code>0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8</code>","why":"Missing the second <code>1</code> (<code>0 + 1 = 1</code>)."}],"why":"Fibonacci: <code>0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ...</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"To check if <code>n</code> is prime, up to what limit do you actually need to test divisors <code>d</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Only <code>d = 2</code>","why":"<code>9</code> is odd (<code>9 % 2 != 0</code>) and not prime (<code>3 * 3</code>)."},{"h":"<code>d * d &lt;= n</code> (i.e. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\sqrt{n}\"></span>)","ok":1,"why":"If <code>n</code> has a factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"&gt; \\sqrt{n}\"></span>, the other factor is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"&lt; \\sqrt{n}\"></span>."},{"h":"<code>d &lt;= n * 2</code>","why":"Divisors of <code>n</code> cannot exceed <code>n</code>."},{"h":"<code>d = 1</code>","why":"Every integer is divisible by <code>1</code>."}],"why":"Testing <code>d</code> from <code>2</code> while <code>d * d &lt;= n</code> (after <code>if (n &lt; 2) return 0;</code>) is both fast and exact.","tag":"10"},{"q":"When building a menu with <code>switch</code> inside a <code>do-while</code> loop, what should the loop condition be?","opts":[{"h":"<code>while (choice == 0);</code>","why":"That repeats <em>only</em> when the user chose Exit (<code>0</code>)!"},{"h":"<code>while (choice != 0);</code>","ok":1,"why":"Keep looping as long as the user has not chosen Exit (<code>0</code>)."},{"h":"<code>while (1);</code> without any <code>break</code>","why":"Infinite loop."},{"h":"<code>while (choice &gt; 0);</code>","why":"Negative invalid choices would exit instead of showing \"Invalid\"."}],"why":"Repeat <code>while (choice != EXIT_CODE);</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"What does a <strong>function prototype</strong> at the top of a <code>.c</code> file end with?","opts":[{"h":"<code>{ ... }</code>","why":"That's a function definition."},{"h":"<code>;</code>","ok":1,"why":"A prototype is a declaration ending with a semicolon, e.g. <code>int add(int a, int b);</code>."},{"h":"<code>:</code>","why":"C does not use colons for prototypes."},{"h":"<code>return 0;</code>","why":"Statements go inside the definition body."}],"why":"Prototype: <code>return_type name(param_types);</code>.","tag":"11"},{"q":"By default, how are regular <code>int</code> arguments passed to functions in C?","opts":[{"h":"Pass-by-reference","why":"Only when you explicitly pass pointers (<code>&amp;x</code>)."},{"h":"Pass-by-value (a copy is passed)","ok":1,"why":"Changes to the parameter do not affect the caller's variable."},{"h":"Pass-by-name","why":"Not used in C."},{"h":"Global sharing","why":"Parameters are local to the function."}],"why":"Regular variables are passed by value (copied).","tag":"11"},{"q":"What happens if a <code>void</code> function tries to execute <code>return 5;</code>?","opts":[{"h":"It returns <code>5</code> anyway","why":"<code>void</code> functions cannot return a value."},{"h":"Compile error","ok":1,"why":"A <code>void</code> function may only use <code>return;</code> with no value."},{"h":"It prints <code>5</code>","why":"<code>return</code> does not print."},{"h":"It sets <code>main</code>'s return to <code>5</code>","why":"<code>return</code> only exits the current function."}],"why":"<code>void</code> means \"returns nothing\", so <code>return &lt;expr&gt;;</code> is an error.","tag":"11"},{"q":"What are the two required parts of a recursive function?","opts":[{"h":"Two <code>for</code> loops","why":"Recursion replaces loops with self-calls."},{"h":"A <strong>base case</strong> (stopping condition) and a <strong>recursive step</strong> moving toward the base case","ok":1,"why":"Without both, it recurses forever."},{"h":"A global variable and a <code>switch</code>","why":"Not required."},{"h":"<code>malloc</code> and <code>free</code>","why":"Dynamic memory is not required for recursion."}],"why":"Base case + recursive step toward the base case.","tag":"11"},{"q":"Trace <code>int f(int n) { return n &lt;= 1 ? 1 : n + f(n - 1); }</code> for <code>f(4)</code>.","opts":[{"h":"<code>24</code>","why":"It uses <code>+</code>, not <code>*</code>!"},{"h":"<code>10</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10</code>."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","why":"Adds all steps down to 1."},{"h":"<code>6</code>","why":"<code>3 + 2 + 1 = 6</code>, missing <code>4 +</code>."}],"why":"<code>f(4) = 4 + f(3) = 4 + 3 + f(2) = 4 + 3 + 2 + f(1) = 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10</code>.","tag":"11"},{"q":"For <code>int a[10];</code>, which indices are valid?","opts":[{"h":"<code>1</code> to <code>10</code>","why":"C arrays start at <code>0</code>, and <code>a[10]</code> is out of bounds!"},{"h":"<code>0</code> to <code>9</code>","ok":1,"why":"Valid indices are <code>0 .. size - 1</code>."},{"h":"<code>0</code> to <code>10</code>","why":"<code>a[10]</code> is one past the end (out of bounds)."},{"h":"<code>-1</code> to <code>9</code>","why":"Negative indices access memory before the array."}],"why":"<code>N</code> elements → indices <code>0</code> through <code>N - 1</code>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"What happens if you write <code>int a[4] = {10, 20};</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Compile error because 2 elements are missing","why":"Partial initialization is legal in C."},{"h":"<code>a[0]=10, a[1]=20</code>, and the remaining elements <code>a[2], a[3]</code> are automatically set to <code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"Any unspecified elements in a partial initializer are zero-filled."},{"h":"<code>a[2]</code> and <code>a[3]</code> hold garbage","why":"Only completely uninitialized local arrays hold garbage."},{"h":"<code>a</code> shrinks to size 2","why":"Its size remains 4."}],"why":"Partial array initialization (<code>int a[4] = {0};</code> is a famous trick) sets remaining elements to <code>0</code>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"In <strong>bubble sort</strong> of an array of size <code>n</code>, why is the inner loop limit <code>n - 1 - i</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Because <code>a[n]</code> is <code>0</code>","why":"<code>a[n]</code> is out of bounds."},{"h":"Because after each pass <code>i</code>, the largest <code>i</code> elements are already in their final positions at the end, and we compare <code>a[j]</code> with <code>a[j + 1]</code>","ok":1,"why":"Avoids out-of-bounds <code>a[j + 1]</code> and skips already-sorted tail elements."},{"h":"To sort in descending order","why":"Comparison <code>&gt;</code> vs <code>&lt;</code> controls order."},{"h":"To skip <code>a[0]</code>","why":"<code>j</code> starts at <code>0</code>."}],"why":"Stopping at <code>n - 1 - i</code> ensures <code>j + 1 &lt; n</code> and avoids re-checking sorted elements at the end.","tag":"12"},{"q":"What does linear search return by convention when the target value is NOT in the array?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"<code>0</code> is a valid array index (the first element)!"},{"h":"<code>-1</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>-1</code> is never a valid index, so it clearly means \"not found\"."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"<code>1</code> is a valid index."},{"h":"<code>n</code>","why":"<code>-1</code> is the standard sentinel."}],"why":"Return <code>-1</code> when not found, because <code>0 .. n-1</code> are valid indices.","tag":"12"},{"q":"How do you pass a 1D <code>int</code> array <code>scores</code> of size <code>20</code> to a function <code>avg</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>avg(scores[20], 20)</code>","why":"<code>scores[20]</code> passes a single out-of-bounds <code>int</code>, not the array!"},{"h":"<code>avg(scores, 20)</code>","ok":1,"why":"Passing the array name passes the address of <code>scores[0]</code>, and <code>20</code> tells the function its length."},{"h":"<code>avg(&amp;scores[], 20)</code>","why":"Invalid syntax."},{"h":"<code>avg(int scores, 20)</code>","why":"Don't put the type in a function call."}],"why":"Pass the array name (<code>scores</code>) and its size (<code>20</code>).","tag":"12"},{"q":"What does the <code>&amp;</code> operator do in <code>int *p = &amp;x;</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Dereferences <code>p</code>","why":"<code>*p</code> dereferences."},{"h":"Gets the <strong>memory address</strong> of <code>x</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>&amp;x</code> means \"address of <code>x</code>\"."},{"h":"Bitwise AND","why":"Unary <code>&amp;x</code> is address-of; binary <code>a &amp; b</code> is bitwise AND."},{"h":"Multiplies <code>x</code>","why":"<code>*</code> multiplies."}],"why":"<code>&amp;var</code> returns the memory address of <code>var</code>.","tag":"13"},{"q":"Given <code>int x = 10; int *p = &amp;x; *p = 30;</code>, what is <code>x</code> now?","opts":[{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"<code>*p = 30</code> writes through <code>p</code> directly into <code>x</code>."},{"h":"<code>30</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>p</code> points to <code>x</code>, so <code>*p</code> <em>is</em> <code>x</code>."},{"h":"Address of <code>p</code>","why":"<code>x</code> holds <code>30</code>."},{"h":"Compile error","why":"Valid pointer dereference."}],"why":"Dereferencing <code>p</code> (<code>*p = 30</code>) modifies the variable <code>p</code> points to (<code>x</code>).","tag":"13"},{"q":"What is the danger of <code>int *p; *p = 5;</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>p</code> is set to <code>0</code>","why":"Uninitialized local pointers hold random garbage addresses!"},{"h":"<code>p</code> holds a garbage address, so writing to <code>*p</code> causes undefined behavior / crash","ok":1,"why":"Always point <code>p</code> at a valid variable (or <code>NULL</code>) first."},{"h":"<code>5</code> becomes a <code>double</code>","why":"Unrelated."},{"h":"It only works in <code>void</code> functions","why":"It is unsafe everywhere."}],"why":"Never dereference an uninitialized or <code>NULL</code> pointer.","tag":"13"},{"q":"How do you declare TWO pointers to <code>int</code> on one line?","opts":[{"h":"<code>int* p, q;</code>","why":"Here <code>p</code> is <code>int *</code>, but <code>q</code> is a plain <code>int</code>!"},{"h":"<code>int *p, *q;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Both <code>p</code> and <code>q</code> have <code>*</code>."},{"h":"<code>*int p, q;</code>","why":"Invalid syntax."},{"h":"<code>pointer p, q;</code>","why":"C has no <code>pointer</code> keyword."}],"why":"Put <code>*</code> before each pointer variable name: <code>int *p, *q;</code>.","tag":"13"},{"q":"On a 64-bit computer, how large is <code>sizeof(int *)</code> vs <code>sizeof(char *)</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>4</code> vs <code>1</code>","why":"Those are <code>sizeof(int)</code> and <code>sizeof(char)</code>, not pointer sizes!"},{"h":"Both are <code>8</code> bytes","ok":1,"why":"Every memory address on a 64-bit system is 8 bytes, regardless of what type it points to."},{"h":"<code>16</code> vs <code>8</code>","why":"All data pointers have the same size on a given architecture."},{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"Pointers occupy memory too."}],"why":"A pointer stores a memory address, so <code>int *</code>, <code>char *</code>, <code>double *</code> all have the same size (8 bytes on 64-bit).","tag":"13"},{"q":"Why does <code>void swap(int *a, int *b)</code> succeed where <code>void swap(int a, int b)</code> fails?","opts":[{"h":"Pointers are faster","why":"Not the reason."},{"h":"Pointers receive the <strong>addresses</strong> of the caller's variables, so <code>*a</code> and <code>*b</code> modify the originals in <code>main</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>int a, b</code> only swaps local copies."},{"h":"<code>int *</code> ignores <code>return</code>","why":"Both are <code>void</code>."},{"h":"<code>int a, b</code> cannot hold integers","why":"They hold copies."}],"why":"Pass-by-reference (via pointers) lets a function change variables in the caller.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Given <code>int arr[5] = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}; int *p = arr;</code>, what is <code>*(p + 3)</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>5</code>","why":"Check the values in <code>arr</code>."},{"h":"<code>6</code>","why":"<code>*(p + 2)</code> is <code>6</code>."},{"h":"<code>8</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>p + 3</code> points to <code>arr[3]</code>, which is <code>8</code>."},{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"<code>*(p + 4)</code> is <code>10</code>."}],"why":"<code>*(p + i)</code> is equivalent to <code>arr[i]</code>.","tag":"14"},{"q":"If <code>double *p</code> holds address <code>0x2000</code> and <code>sizeof(double) == 8</code>, what address is <code>p + 2</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0x2002</code>","why":"Pointer arithmetic scales by <code>sizeof(double)</code>."},{"h":"<code>0x2010</code> (<code>0x2000 + 2 * 8 = 0x2000 + 16</code>)","ok":1,"why":"<code>16</code> in hex is <code>0x10</code>, so <code>0x2000 + 0x10 = 0x2010</code>."},{"h":"<code>0x2008</code>","why":"That is <code>p + 1</code>."},{"h":"<code>0x2020</code>","why":"That would be <code>p + 4</code>."}],"why":"<code>p + k</code> advances by <code>k * sizeof(*p)</code> bytes.","tag":"14"},{"q":"What is the difference between <code>(*p)++</code> and <code>*p++</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Identical","why":"Operator precedence differs!"},{"h":"<code>(*p)++</code> increments the <strong>value</strong> pointed to by <code>p</code>; <code>*p++</code> reads <code>*p</code> and advances the <strong>pointer</strong> <code>p</code> to the next element","ok":1,"why":"<code>++</code> binds tighter than <code>*</code>."},{"h":"<code>*p++</code> is a syntax error","why":"Both are valid C."},{"h":"<code>(*p)++</code> advances <code>p</code> twice","why":"Only increments the target value once."}],"why":"Use parentheses <code>(*p)++</code> when you want to increment the value at <code>p</code>.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which expression is equivalent to <code>arr[i]</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>*arr + i</code>","why":"Adds <code>i</code> to <code>arr[0]</code>!"},{"h":"<code>*(arr + i)</code>","ok":1,"why":"Pointer arithmetic then dereference."},{"h":"<code>&amp;arr + i</code>","why":"Advances by <code>i</code> whole arrays."},{"h":"<code>arr * i</code>","why":"Invalid on pointers."}],"why":"<code>arr[i]</code> is defined in C as <code>*(arr + i)</code>.","tag":"14"},{"q":"What marks the end of every C string?","opts":[{"h":"<code>\\n</code>","why":"Newline is just a normal character inside text."},{"h":"<code>'\\0'</code> (null character, ASCII <code>0</code>)","ok":1,"why":"String functions stop at <code>'\\0'</code>."},{"h":"<code>' '</code>","why":"Spaces can appear inside strings."},{"h":"<code>EOF</code>","why":"<code>EOF</code> is for file/input streams, not inside strings."}],"why":"C strings are null-terminated char arrays ending in <code>'\\0'</code>.","tag":"15"},{"q":"What does <code>strcmp(\"apple\", \"apple\")</code> return?","opts":[{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"Non-zero means <em>not</em> equal!"},{"h":"<code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>strcmp</code> returns <code>0</code> when strings match exactly."},{"h":"<code>-1</code>","why":"Negative means first &lt; second."},{"h":"<code>5</code>","why":"<code>strlen</code> returns <code>5</code>."}],"why":"<code>strcmp(a, b) == 0</code> tests string equality.","tag":"15"},{"q":"What does <code>strlen(\"Code\")</code> return?","opts":[{"h":"<code>5</code>","why":"<code>strlen</code> does NOT count <code>'\\0'</code>."},{"h":"<code>4</code>","ok":1,"why":"Counts <code>'C', 'o', 'd', 'e'</code> before <code>'\\0'</code>."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","why":"Counts all 4 letters."},{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"Only <code>\"\"</code> has length <code>0</code>."}],"why":"<code>strlen</code> counts characters before <code>'\\0'</code>; <code>sizeof(\"Code\")</code> is <code>5</code> (includes <code>'\\0'</code>).","tag":"15"},{"q":"Which <code>&lt;ctype.h&gt;</code> function checks if <code>char c</code> is <code>'0'..'9'</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>isalpha(c)</code>","why":"Checks letters <code>A-Z</code>, <code>a-z</code>."},{"h":"<code>isdigit(c)</code>","ok":1,"why":"Returns non-zero if <code>c</code> is a digit <code>'0'..'9'</code>."},{"h":"<code>toupper(c)</code>","why":"Converts lowercase to uppercase."},{"h":"<code>strlen(c)</code>","why":"<code>strlen</code> is for strings, not a single <code>char</code>."}],"why":"<code>&lt;ctype.h&gt;</code> provides <code>isdigit</code>, <code>isalpha</code>, <code>isalnum</code>, <code>toupper</code>, <code>tolower</code>.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Why is <code>char name[10]; name = \"Deniz\";</code> a compile error?","opts":[{"h":"<code>\"Deniz\"</code> is too long","why":"<code>\"Deniz\"</code> needs 6 bytes, which fits in 10."},{"h":"Arrays cannot be assigned with <code>=</code> after declaration; use <code>strcpy(name, \"Deniz\");</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>=</code> only works in the initialization <code>char name[10] = \"Deniz\";</code>."},{"h":"Missing <code>&amp;</code>","why":"Arrays don't use <code>&amp;</code> here."},{"h":"<code>char</code> cannot hold letters","why":"<code>char</code> holds letters."}],"why":"Use <code>strcpy(dst, src)</code> from <code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code> to copy into an existing <code>char</code> array.","tag":"15"},{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"%-6s!\", \"Hi\");</code> print (using <code>·</code> for spaces)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>····Hi!</code>","why":"Without <code>-</code>, it right-aligns."},{"h":"<code>Hi····!</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>-6s</code> left-aligns <code>\"Hi\"</code> in 6 columns, padding 4 spaces on the right."},{"h":"<code>Hi!</code>","why":"Minimum width <code>6</code> adds 4 spaces."},{"h":"<code>0000Hi!</code>","why":"<code>%s</code> pads with spaces, not zeros."}],"why":"<code>-</code> left-aligns the field.","tag":"16"},{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"%04d\", 7);</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>   7</code>","why":"Space-padded is <code>%4d</code>."},{"h":"<code>0007</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>0</code> flag pads with leading zeros to width 4."},{"h":"<code>7000</code>","why":"Padding is on the left, not right."},{"h":"<code>7.000</code>","why":"<code>%d</code> is integer, no decimal point."}],"why":"<code>%04d</code> pads integers with leading zeros up to width 4.","tag":"16"},{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"%.2f\", 5.678);</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"<code>5.67</code>","why":"<code>printf</code> rounds, it does not truncate!"},{"h":"<code>5.68</code>","ok":1,"why":"Rounded to 2 decimal places."},{"h":"<code>5.6</code>","why":"<code>.2f</code> prints 2 decimal digits."},{"h":"<code>05.68</code>","why":"No leading zero width specified."}],"why":"<code>printf</code> rounds floating-point output to the requested precision.","tag":"16"},{"q":"What does <code>while (scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) == 1)</code> do?","opts":[{"h":"Reads exactly 1 integer and stops","why":"<code>== 1</code> means \"1 item read on <em>each</em> pass\"."},{"h":"Keeps reading integers until input ends or a non-integer is encountered","ok":1,"why":"<code>scanf</code> returns the count of items successfully read."},{"h":"Loops forever","why":"Stops when <code>scanf</code> returns <code>0</code> or <code>EOF</code>."},{"h":"Prints <code>1</code>","why":"<code>scanf</code> reads, it doesn't print."}],"why":"Checking <code>scanf(...) == N</code> is the standard way to verify <code>N</code> items were read.","tag":"16"},{"q":"What does <code>int k = printf(\"OK\\n\");</code> assign to <code>k</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>2</code>","why":"<code>\\n</code> is also 1 character."},{"h":"<code>3</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>'O'</code>, <code>'K'</code>, <code>'\\n'</code> = 3 characters printed."},{"h":"<code>0</code>","why":"<code>printf</code> returns character count, not <code>0</code>."},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"Returns total characters, not lines."}],"why":"<code>printf</code> returns the number of characters printed.","tag":"16"}],"exercises":[{"id":"c-05-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Print a two-line greeting","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>Write a C program that prints: <code>Hello, IGU!</code> on the first line and <code>Semester 1</code> on the second line.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"Hello, IGU!\\n\");\n    printf(\"Semester 1\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Use two <code>printf</code> calls (or one with <code>\\n</code> in the middle)."},{"id":"c-05-02","diff":"easy","kind":"fill","title":"Declare and print variables","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks so the program declares an <code>int</code> named <code>credits</code> with value <code>30</code> and a <code>char</code> named <code>grade</code> with value <code>'A'</code>, then prints <code>30 A</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int credits = 30;\n    char grade = 'A';\n    printf(\"%d %c\\n\", credits, grade);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    ____ credits = 30;\n    ____ grade = 'A';\n    printf(\"%d %c\\n\", ____, ____);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Whole numbers use <code>int</code>, single characters use <code>char</code>."},{"id":"c-05-03","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix the char quote bug","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>This program fails to compile because of wrong quotes and a missing semicolon. Fix it so it prints <code>Level: B</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char level = 'B';\n    printf(\"Level: %c\\n\", level);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char level = \"B\"\n    printf(\"Level: %c\\n\", level);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Single characters need single quotes <code>'B'</code>, and statements end with <code>;</code>."},{"id":"c-05-04","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict variable values","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    int b = a + 3;\n    a = 10;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", a, b);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-05-05","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Rectangle area and perimeter","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>Declare <code>int width = 7, height = 4;</code>. Compute and print: <code>Area: 28</code> <code>Perimeter: 22</code></p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int width = 7, height = 4;\n    printf(\"Area: %d\\n\", width * height);\n    printf(\"Perimeter: %d\\n\", 2 * (width + height));\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Perimeter is <code>2 * (width + height)</code>."},{"id":"c-05-06","diff":"medium","kind":"bug","title":"Fix the invalid variable name","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>Fix the variable name and missing initialization so it prints <code>Score: 95</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int score1 = 95;\n    printf(\"Score: %d\\n\", score1);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int 1score = 95;\n    printf(\"Score: %d\\n\", 1score);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Variable names cannot start with a digit."},{"id":"c-05-07","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict sizeof output","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>What does this program print? (On 64-bit systems <code>int</code> is 4 bytes, <code>double</code> is 8 bytes, <code>char</code> is 1 byte.)</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", (int)sizeof(char), (int)sizeof(int), (int)sizeof(double));\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-05-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Swap two variables using a temporary","lesson":"c-05","prompt":"<p>Write a program with <code>int a = 12, b = 34;</code>. Swap their values using a temporary variable <code>tmp</code>, then print <code>a=34 b=12</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 12, b = 34;\n    int tmp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = tmp;\n    printf(\"a=%d b=%d\\n\", a, b);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","checklist":["Declares <code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, and a third <code>tmp</code> variable","Performs the 3-step swap <code>tmp = a; a = b; b = tmp;</code>","Prints <code>a=34 b=12</code> with <code>\\n</code>"]},{"id":"c-06-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Read and double an integer","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>Read one integer <code>n</code> from input and print <code>2 * n</code> followed by a newline.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", 2 * n);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"15","hint":"Remember <code>&amp;n</code> in <code>scanf</code>."},{"id":"c-06-02","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix the missing & in scanf","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>Fix the bug so the program reads two integers and prints their sum.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b);\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a + b);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", a, b);\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a + b);\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"8 13","hint":"<code>scanf</code> needs <code>&amp;a</code> and <code>&amp;b</code>."},{"id":"c-06-03","diff":"easy","kind":"fill","title":"Read a double and print 2 decimals","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks to read a <code>double x</code> and print it with 2 decimal places.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    double x;\n    scanf(\"%lf\", &x);\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", x);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    double x;\n    scanf(\"____\", &x);\n    printf(\"____\\n\", x);\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"3.14159","hint":"<code>scanf</code> uses <code>%lf</code> for <code>double</code>; <code>printf</code> uses <code>%.2f</code>."},{"id":"c-06-04","diff":"medium","kind":"bug","title":"Fix double scanf specifier","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>This program uses <code>%f</code> to read a <code>double</code>. Fix it so <code>7.5</code> prints as <code>15.00</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    double x;\n    scanf(\"%lf\", &x);\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", x * 2);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    double x;\n    scanf(\"%f\", &x);\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", x * 2);\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"7.5","hint":"Change <code>%f</code> to <code>%lf</code> in <code>scanf</code>."},{"id":"c-06-05","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict printf with tabs and percent","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int p = 85;\n    printf(\"Score:\\t%d%%\\n\", p);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-06-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Read int and char safely","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>Read an integer <code>n</code> and a character <code>c</code> (separated by newline in input) and print <code>n=4 c=K</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    char c;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    scanf(\" %c\", &c);\n    printf(\"n=%d c=%c\\n\", n, c);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"4\nK","hint":"Put a space before <code>%c</code> (<code>\" %c\"</code>) to skip the newline after <code>4</code>."},{"id":"c-06-07","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Average of three integers","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>Read 3 integers and print their average with 2 decimal places.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b, c;\n    scanf(\"%d %d %d\", &a, &b, &c);\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", (a + b + c) / 3.0);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"10 15 20","hint":"Divide by <code>3.0</code> (not <code>3</code>) so you don't lose the decimal part."},{"id":"c-06-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Temperature converter (C to F)","lesson":"c-06","prompt":"<p>Read a temperature in Celsius as a <code>double</code> and print Fahrenheit (<code>F = C * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0</code>) formatted as <code>F = 77.00</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    double c, f;\n    scanf(\"%lf\", &c);\n    f = c * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0;\n    printf(\"F = %.2f\\n\", f);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"25.0","checklist":["Uses <code>double</code> and reads with <code>%lf</code>","Uses floating-point division <code>9.0 / 5.0</code>","Prints <code>F = %.2f\\n</code>"]},{"id":"c-07-01","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict integer division and modulo","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 17, b = 5;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", a / b, a % b);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-07-02","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict prefix and postfix ++","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int i = 4;\n    int a = i++;\n    int b = ++i;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", i, a, b);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-07-03","diff":"easy","kind":"fill","title":"Fix integer division with a cast","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blank so <code>7 / 2</code> prints <code>3.50</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 7, b = 2;\n    double res = (double)a / b;\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", res);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 7, b = 2;\n    double res = ____a / b;\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", res);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Cast <code>a</code> to <code>(double)</code> before dividing."},{"id":"c-07-04","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict operator precedence","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 2 + 3 * 4 - 10 / 4;\n    int y = (2 + 3) * (4 - 1);\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-07-05","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict logical and short-circuit output","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 0, b = 5;\n    int r = (a && (b = 99));\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", r, b);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-07-06","diff":"medium","kind":"bug","title":"Fix chained comparison bug","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>Fix the condition so it prints <code>In range</code> only when <code>x</code> is between <code>10</code> and <code>20</code> inclusive (for <code>x = 25</code>, it should print <code>Out</code>).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    if (x >= 10 && x <= 20) {\n        printf(\"In range\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Out\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    if (10 <= x <= 20) {\n        printf(\"In range\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Out\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"25","hint":"Split <code>10 &lt;= x &lt;= 20</code> using <code>&amp;&amp;</code>."},{"id":"c-07-07","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Split total minutes into hours and minutes","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>Read <code>total</code> minutes (an integer) and print hours and remaining minutes as <code>2h 15m</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int total;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &total);\n    printf(\"%dh %dm\\n\", total / 60, total % 60);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"135","hint":"Use <code>/</code> for hours and <code>%</code> for remaining minutes."},{"id":"c-07-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Sum of digits of a 3-digit number","lesson":"c-07","prompt":"<p>Read a 3-digit integer <code>n</code> (e.g. <code>472</code>) and print the sum of its digits (<code>13</code>) using <code>/</code> and <code>%</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    int d1 = n / 100;\n    int d2 = (n / 10) % 10;\n    int d3 = n % 10;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", d1 + d2 + d3);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"472","checklist":["Extracts hundreds digit with <code>n / 100</code>","Extracts tens digit with <code>(n / 10) % 10</code>","Extracts ones digit with <code>n % 10</code> and prints the sum"]},{"id":"c-08-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Even or odd","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>Read an integer <code>n</code>. Print <code>Even</code> if it is even, or <code>Odd</code> if it is odd.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    if (n % 2 == 0) {\n        printf(\"Even\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Odd\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"19","hint":"Check <code>n % 2 == 0</code>."},{"id":"c-08-02","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix assignment = instead of ==","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>Fix the bug so <code>x = 3</code> prints <code>Not five</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    if (x == 5) {\n        printf(\"Five\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Not five\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    if (x = 5) {\n        printf(\"Five\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Not five\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"3","hint":"Comparison in C is <code>==</code>, not <code>=</code>."},{"id":"c-08-03","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix missing break in switch","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>Fix the <code>switch</code> so input <code>2</code> prints only <code>Two</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    switch (n) {\n        case 1: printf(\"One\\n\"); break;\n        case 2: printf(\"Two\\n\"); break;\n        case 3: printf(\"Three\\n\"); break;\n        default: printf(\"Other\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    switch (n) {\n        case 1: printf(\"One\\n\"); break;\n        case 2: printf(\"Two\\n\");\n        case 3: printf(\"Three\\n\"); break;\n        default: printf(\"Other\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"2","hint":"Add <code>break;</code> at the end of <code>case 2</code>."},{"id":"c-08-04","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict else-if chain","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int s;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &s);\n    if (s >= 90) printf(\"A\");\n    else if (s >= 70) printf(\"B\");\n    if (s >= 50) printf(\"C\");\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"75"},{"id":"c-08-05","diff":"medium","kind":"fill","title":"Ternary operator max","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blank using the ternary operator <code>?:</code> to store the larger of <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> in <code>m</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b);\n    int m = (a > b) ? a : b;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", m);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b);\n    int m = ____;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", m);\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"12 27","hint":"Syntax: <code>(a &gt; b) ? a : b</code>."},{"id":"c-08-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Simple switch calculator","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>Read two integers <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> and an operator <code>op</code> (<code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>) on one line (e.g. <code>10 3 +</code>). Use <code>switch (op)</code> to print the integer result (for <code>/</code>, print <code>Zero</code> if <code>b == 0</code>).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    char op;\n    scanf(\"%d %d %c\", &a, &b, &op);\n    switch (op) {\n        case '+': printf(\"%d\\n\", a + b); break;\n        case '-': printf(\"%d\\n\", a - b); break;\n        case '*': printf(\"%d\\n\", a * b); break;\n        case '/':\n            if (b == 0) printf(\"Zero\\n\");\n            else printf(\"%d\\n\", a / b);\n            break;\n        default: printf(\"Invalid\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"12 4 *","hint":"Remember <code> %c</code> in <code>scanf</code> and <code>break;</code> after each <code>case</code>."},{"id":"c-08-07","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Largest of three numbers","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>Read 3 integers and print the largest one.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b, c;\n    scanf(\"%d %d %d\", &a, &b, &c);\n    int max = a;\n    if (b > max) max = b;\n    if (c > max) max = c;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", max);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"14 29 21","hint":"Start with <code>max = a</code>, then check <code>b</code> and <code>c</code>."},{"id":"c-08-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Leap year check","lesson":"c-08","prompt":"<p>Read a year <code>y</code>. Print <code>Leap</code> if it is divisible by 400, OR divisible by 4 and NOT divisible by 100; otherwise print <code>Normal</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int y;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &y);\n    if ((y % 400 == 0) || (y % 4 == 0 && y % 100 != 0)) {\n        printf(\"Leap\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Normal\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"2024","checklist":["Checks <code>y % 400 == 0</code>","Checks <code>y % 4 == 0 &amp;&amp; y % 100 != 0</code>","Combines conditions with <code>||</code> and prints <code>Leap</code> / <code>Normal</code>"]},{"id":"c-09-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Sum 1 to N","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>Read a positive integer <code>n</code> and print the sum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"1 + 2 + \\dots + n\"></span>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, sum = 0;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"10","hint":"Initialize <code>sum = 0</code> and loop <code>i</code> from <code>1</code> to <code>n</code>."},{"id":"c-09-02","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix off-by-one loop bound","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>This program should print <code>1 2 3 4 5</code> (with a trailing space), but it stops at <code>4</code>. Fix the bug.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Change <code>&lt; 5</code> to <code>&lt;= 5</code>."},{"id":"c-09-03","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix empty loop semicolon","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>Fix the extra semicolon so the loop prints <code>0 1 2 3</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int i;\n    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++); {\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Remove the <code>;</code> right after <code>for (...)</code>."},{"id":"c-09-04","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict while loop with continue and break","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < 6) {\n        i++;\n        if (i == 2) continue;\n        if (i == 5) break;\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-09-05","diff":"medium","kind":"fill","title":"Factorial using a for loop","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks to compute <code>n!</code> (<code>5! = 120</code>).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, fact = 1;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        fact *= i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", fact);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, fact = ____;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        fact ____ i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", fact);\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"5","hint":"Start <code>fact</code> at <code>1</code> and multiply by <code>i</code> (<code>*=</code>)."},{"id":"c-09-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Sentinel while loop (stop on 0)","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>Read integers until <code>0</code> is entered, then print the sum of all positive numbers entered before <code>0</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x, sum = 0;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    while (x != 0) {\n        if (x > 0) sum += x;\n        scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"4 -2 7 3 0","hint":"Read the first <code>x</code> before <code>while (x != 0)</code>, and read the next <code>x</code> at the bottom of the loop."},{"id":"c-09-07","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict do-while output","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    do {\n        printf(\"%d \", x);\n        x -= 4;\n    } while (x > 5);\n    printf(\"end=%d\\n\", x);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-09-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Average of N numbers","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>Read an integer <code>N</code>, then read <code>N</code> integers and print their average with 2 decimal places (<code>Avg: 15.50</code>).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, x, sum = 0;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    printf(\"Avg: %.2f\\n\", (double)sum / n);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"4 10 20 12 20","checklist":["Reads <code>N</code> first, then loops <code>N</code> times","Accumulates <code>sum</code> initialized to <code>0</code>","Casts to <code>(double)</code> before dividing by <code>n</code> and formats with <code>%.2f</code>"]},{"id":"c-09-09","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Multiplication table row","lesson":"c-09","prompt":"<p>Read <code>n</code> and print <code>n * 1</code> through <code>n * 5</code> on separate lines like <code>3 x 1 = 3</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d x %d = %d\\n\", n, i, n * i);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"3","hint":"Loop <code>i</code> from <code>1</code> to <code>5</code>."},{"id":"c-10-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Right-triangle star pattern","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Read <code>n</code> and print a right triangle of <code>*</code> with <code>n</code> rows (row <code>i</code> has <code>i</code> stars).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {\n            printf(\"*\");\n        }\n        printf(\"\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"4","hint":"Outer loop <code>i = 1..n</code>, inner loop <code>j = 1..i</code>, then <code>printf(\"\\n\")</code>."},{"id":"c-10-02","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Inverted star triangle","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Read <code>n</code> and print an inverted triangle starting with <code>n</code> stars on row 1 down to <code>1</code> star on row <code>n</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = n; i >= 1; i--) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {\n            printf(\"*\");\n        }\n        printf(\"\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"4","hint":"Count outer loop <code>i</code> down from <code>n</code> to <code>1</code>."},{"id":"c-10-03","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Sum of digits","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Read a positive integer <code>n</code> and print the sum of its digits.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, sum = 0;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    while (n > 0) {\n        sum += n % 10;\n        n /= 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"5083","hint":"Use <code>n % 10</code> to get the last digit and <code>n /= 10</code> to remove it."},{"id":"c-10-04","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Reverse a number","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Read a positive integer <code>n</code> and print its digits reversed (e.g. <code>1234</code> → <code>4321</code>).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, rev = 0;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    while (n > 0) {\n        rev = rev * 10 + (n % 10);\n        n /= 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", rev);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"1234","hint":"Shift <code>rev</code> left by multiplying by <code>10</code>, then add <code>n % 10</code>."},{"id":"c-10-05","diff":"medium","kind":"bug","title":"Fix prime checker for n = 1 and early print","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Fix this prime checker so <code>1</code> prints <code>Not prime</code> and <code>17</code> prints <code>Prime</code> once.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, isPrime = 1;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    if (n < 2) isPrime = 0;\n    for (int d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) {\n        if (n % d == 0) {\n            isPrime = 0;\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    if (isPrime) printf(\"Prime\\n\");\n    else printf(\"Not prime\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, isPrime = 1;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int d = 2; d < n; d++) {\n        if (n % d == 0) isPrime = 0;\n    }\n    if (isPrime) printf(\"Prime\\n\");\n    else printf(\"Not prime\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"1","hint":"Numbers <code>&lt; 2</code> (like <code>1</code> and <code>0</code>) are not prime!"},{"id":"c-10-06","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict nested loop output","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {\n            printf(\"%d\", j);\n        }\n        printf(\" \");\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-10-07","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"First N Fibonacci numbers","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Read <code>N</code> (<code>N &gt;= 1</code>) and print the first <code>N</code> Fibonacci numbers starting <code>0 1 1 2 3 ...</code> separated by spaces.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    int a = 0, b = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d \", a);\n        int next = a + b;\n        a = b;\n        b = next;\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"7","hint":"Keep <code>a = 0, b = 1</code>, print <code>a</code>, then advance <code>next = a + b; a = b; b = next;</code>."},{"id":"c-10-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"GCD using Euclid's algorithm","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Read two positive integers <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> and print their Greatest Common Divisor (GCD).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b);\n    while (b != 0) {\n        int r = a % b;\n        a = b;\n        b = r;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"48 18","checklist":["Loops while <code>b != 0</code>","Computes remainder <code>r = a % b</code>, shifts <code>a = b; b = r;</code>","Prints <code>a</code> after the loop ends"]},{"id":"c-10-09","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Menu loop with switch","lesson":"c-10","prompt":"<p>Read menu choices (<code>1</code> prints <code>Add</code>, <code>2</code> prints <code>Sub</code>, <code>0</code> prints <code>Exit</code> and stops).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int ch;\n    do {\n        scanf(\"%d\", &ch);\n        switch (ch) {\n            case 1: printf(\"Add\\n\"); break;\n            case 2: printf(\"Sub\\n\"); break;\n            case 0: printf(\"Exit\\n\"); break;\n            default: printf(\"?\\n\");\n        }\n    } while (ch != 0);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"1 2 9 0","hint":"Use <code>do { ... } while (ch != 0);</code> with <code>switch (ch)</code>."},{"id":"c-11-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Write a max2 function","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>Write a function <code>int max2(int a, int b)</code> that returns the larger of <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>. Read two integers in <code>main</code> and print <code>max2(a, b)</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint max2(int a, int b) {\n    return (a > b) ? a : b;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b);\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", max2(a, b));\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"18 42","hint":"Define <code>max2</code> above <code>main</code> (or put a prototype above <code>main</code>)."},{"id":"c-11-02","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix missing return statement","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>Fix the function <code>square</code> so <code>square(6)</code> returns <code>36</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint square(int n) {\n    return n * n;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", square(6));\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint square(int n) {\n    int r = n * n;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", square(6));\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"An <code>int</code> function must <code>return</code> a value!"},{"id":"c-11-03","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict pass-by-value behavior","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid addTen(int x) {\n    x += 10;\n    printf(\"in=%d \", x);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 5;\n    addTen(x);\n    printf(\"out=%d\\n\", x);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-11-04","diff":"medium","kind":"fill","title":"Write a recursive power function","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks so <code>power(2, 5)</code> returns <code>32</code> recursively.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint power(int base, int exp) {\n    if (exp == 0) return 1;\n    return base * power(base, exp - 1);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", power(2, 5));\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint power(int base, int exp) {\n    if (exp == 0) return ____;\n    return base * power(base, ____);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", power(2, 5));\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Base case: anything to the power <code>0</code> is <code>1</code>; recursive step reduces <code>exp</code> by <code>1</code>."},{"id":"c-11-05","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict recursive factorial trace","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint f(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * f(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", f(4), f(0));\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-11-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Write an isPrime function","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>Write <code>int isPrime(int n)</code> returning <code>1</code> if <code>n</code> is prime and <code>0</code> otherwise. Read <code>n</code> in <code>main</code> and print <code>Yes</code> or <code>No</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint isPrime(int n) {\n    if (n < 2) return 0;\n    for (int d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) {\n        if (n % d == 0) return 0;\n    }\n    return 1;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", isPrime(n) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"29","hint":"Return <code>0</code> immediately when <code>n &lt; 2</code> or a divisor is found; return <code>1</code> after the loop."},{"id":"c-11-07","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict local vs global scope","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint g = 10;\n\nvoid fn() {\n    int g = 50;\n    printf(\"%d \", g);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    fn();\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", g);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-11-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Recursive Fibonacci function","lesson":"c-11","prompt":"<p>Write <code>int fib(int n)</code> recursively (<code>fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1</code>) and print <code>fib(n)</code> for input <code>n</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint fib(int n) {\n    if (n < 2) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", fib(n));\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"8","checklist":["Base case handles <code>n == 0</code> and <code>n == 1</code>","Recursive return <code>fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)</code>","Reads <code>n</code> and prints result"]},{"id":"c-12-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Sum and average of an array","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>Read <code>5</code> integers into an array, then print their sum and average as <code>Sum=30 Avg=6.0</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5], sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n        scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n        sum += a[i];\n    }\n    printf(\"Sum=%d Avg=%.1f\\n\", sum, sum / 5.0);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"4 8 6 2 10","hint":"Loop <code>i = 0 .. 4</code> to read <code>&amp;a[i]</code> and add to <code>sum</code>."},{"id":"c-12-02","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix array out-of-bounds loop","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>This code accesses <code>a[5]</code> on an array of size <code>5</code>! Fix the loop bound so it prints <code>10 20 30 40 50</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Arrays start at index <code>0</code> and end at <code>n - 1</code> (<code>i &lt; 5</code>)."},{"id":"c-12-03","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Linear search in an array","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>Given <code>int a[6] = {12, 45, 7, 23, 56, 19};</code>, read a <code>target</code> integer and print its index (<code>0..5</code>), or <code>-1</code> if not found.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[6] = {12, 45, 7, 23, 56, 19};\n    int target, pos = -1;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &target);\n    for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {\n        if (a[i] == target) {\n            pos = i;\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", pos);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"23","hint":"Start <code>pos = -1</code> and update <code>pos = i; break;</code> when <code>a[i] == target</code>."},{"id":"c-12-04","diff":"medium","kind":"fill","title":"Bubble sort ascending","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks to sort the array in ascending order.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5] = {5, 1, 4, 2, 3}, n = 5;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {\n            if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {\n                int tmp = a[j];\n                a[j] = a[j + 1];\n                a[j + 1] = tmp;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5] = {5, 1, 4, 2, 3}, n = 5;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {\n            if (a[j] ____ a[j + 1]) {\n                int tmp = a[j];\n                a[j] = ____;\n                a[j + 1] = tmp;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Swap when <code>a[j] &gt; a[j + 1]</code>."},{"id":"c-12-05","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict array passed to a function","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid incFirst(int arr[], int n) {\n    arr[0] += 5;\n    n = 99;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a[3] = {10, 20, 30};\n    int size = 3;\n    incFirst(a, size);\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", a[0], size);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-12-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Reverse print an array","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>Read <code>5</code> integers into an array and print them in reverse order separated by spaces.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5];\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n    for (int i = 4; i >= 0; i--) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"1 2 3 4 5","hint":"Loop <code>i</code> from <code>4</code> down to <code>0</code>."},{"id":"c-12-07","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Sum of a 2D matrix","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>Given a <code>2x3</code> matrix <code>m</code>, print the sum of all 6 elements (<code>21</code>).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int m[2][3] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6} };\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < 2; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) {\n            sum += m[r][c];\n        }\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Use nested loops: <code>r = 0..1</code> and <code>c = 0..2</code>."},{"id":"c-12-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Function returning largest array element","lesson":"c-12","prompt":"<p>Write a function <code>int maxArr(int a[], int n)</code> that returns the largest element in <code>a</code>. Test it on <code>{-5, -2, -9, -1, -7}</code> and print <code>-1</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint maxArr(int a[], int n) {\n    int m = a[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        if (a[i] > m) m = a[i];\n    }\n    return m;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int nums[5] = {-5, -2, -9, -1, -7};\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", maxArr(nums, 5));\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","checklist":["Initializes <code>m = a[0]</code> (NOT <code>0</code>, which fails on negative numbers!)","Loops from <code>i = 1</code> to <code>n - 1</code>","Returns <code>m</code> and calls <code>maxArr(nums, 5)</code> from <code>main</code>"]},{"id":"c-13-01","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict pointer dereference","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    int *p = &x;\n    *p = 42;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, *p);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-13-02","diff":"easy","kind":"fill","title":"Declare and dereference a pointer","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks so <code>p</code> points to <code>a</code> and doubles <code>a</code>'s value through <code>p</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 7;\n    int *p = &a;\n    *p = *p * 2;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 7;\n    int ____p = ____a;\n    *p = *p * 2;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"<code>int *p = &amp;a;</code>"},{"id":"c-13-03","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix uninitialized pointer bug","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p><code>p</code> is <code>NULL</code> (or uninitialized) and never pointed to <code>x</code>. Fix it so it prints <code>50</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 50;\n    int *p = &x;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 50;\n    int *p = NULL;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Assign <code>p = &amp;x;</code> before dereferencing <code>*p</code>."},{"id":"c-13-04","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict two pointers to the same variable","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 5, b = 10;\n    int *p = &a, *q = &b;\n    p = q;\n    *p = 99;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", a, b);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-13-05","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict pointer reassignment","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    int *p = &x;\n    *p += 4;\n    p = &y;\n    *p += 8;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-13-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Add two numbers using pointers","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p>Declare <code>int a = 12, b = 8;</code> and pointers <code>p = &amp;a, q = &amp;b;</code>. Print <code>*p + *q</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 12, b = 8;\n    int *p = &a, *q = &b;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p + *q);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Use <code>*p + *q</code>."},{"id":"c-13-07","diff":"medium","kind":"bug","title":"Fix pointer declaration int* a, b","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p><code>b</code> was declared as an <code>int</code> instead of <code>int *</code>. Fix the declaration so both <code>p</code> and <code>q</code> are pointers and it prints <code>3 7</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 3, y = 7;\n    int *p = &x, *q = &y;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", *p, *q);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 3, y = 7;\n    int* p = &x, q = &y;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", *p, *q);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Put <code>*</code> in front of each pointer variable: <code>int *p = &amp;x, *q = &amp;y;</code>."},{"id":"c-13-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Find min and max via pointer parameters","lesson":"c-13","prompt":"<p>Write <code>void minMax(int a, int b, int *mn, int *mx)</code> that stores the smaller in <code>*mn</code> and larger in <code>*mx</code>. Read two ints and print <code>min=.. max=..</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid minMax(int a, int b, int *mn, int *mx) {\n    if (a < b) {\n        *mn = a;\n        *mx = b;\n    } else {\n        *mn = b;\n        *mx = a;\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int x, y, lo, hi;\n    scanf(\"%d %d\", &x, &y);\n    minMax(x, y, &lo, &hi);\n    printf(\"min=%d max=%d\\n\", lo, hi);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"19 6","checklist":["Function takes <code>int *mn, int *mx</code>","Assigns through <code>*mn = ...</code> and <code>*mx = ...</code>","<code>main</code> passes <code>&amp;lo, &amp;hi</code>"]},{"id":"c-14-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"Write a swap function with pointers","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>Write <code>void swap(int *a, int *b)</code> and call it in <code>main</code> to swap <code>x = 10</code> and <code>y = 25</code>, printing <code>25 10</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) {\n    int tmp = *a;\n    *a = *b;\n    *b = tmp;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 25;\n    swap(&x, &y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Pass <code>&amp;x, &amp;y</code> to <code>swap</code>."},{"id":"c-14-02","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix swap called without &","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>Fix the call to <code>swap</code> so <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> actually swap (<code>9 4</code>).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) {\n    int tmp = *a; *a = *b; *b = tmp;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 4, y = 9;\n    swap(&x, &y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) {\n    int tmp = *a; *a = *b; *b = tmp;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 4, y = 9;\n    swap(x, y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"<code>swap</code> expects addresses: <code>swap(&amp;x, &amp;y);</code>."},{"id":"c-14-03","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict pointer arithmetic on an array","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    int *p = a;\n    p += 2;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", *p, *(p - 1));\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-14-04","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict *p++ vs (*p)++","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[3] = {5, 10, 15};\n    int *p = a;\n    int x = *p++;\n    int y = (*p)++;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d %d\\n\", x, y, a[0], a[1]);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-14-05","diff":"medium","kind":"fill","title":"Sum an array using a pointer","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks to sum <code>a</code> using pointer <code>p</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[4] = {3, 6, 9, 12}, sum = 0;\n    int *p = a;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        sum += *(p + i);\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[4] = {3, 6, 9, 12}, sum = 0;\n    int *p = a;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        sum += ____(p + i);\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Dereference <code>p + i</code> with <code>*</code>."},{"id":"c-14-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Double every element via pointer","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>Write <code>void doubleAll(int *p, int n)</code> that doubles every element of an array using pointer arithmetic. Test on <code>{2, 4, 6}</code> and print <code>4 8 12</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid doubleAll(int *p, int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        *(p + i) *= 2;\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a[3] = {2, 4, 6};\n    doubleAll(a, 3);\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"<code>*(p + i) *= 2;</code>"},{"id":"c-14-07","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Reverse an array in-place with two pointers","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>Use two pointers <code>left = a</code> and <code>right = a + 4</code> to reverse <code>{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}</code> in-place and print <code>5 4 3 2 1</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    int *left = a, *right = a + 4;\n    while (left < right) {\n        int tmp = *left;\n        *left = *right;\n        *right = tmp;\n        left++;\n        right--;\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Swap <code>*left</code> and <code>*right</code>, then <code>left++</code> and <code>right--</code> while <code>left &lt; right</code>."},{"id":"c-14-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Sum and average via pointer output parameter","lesson":"c-14","prompt":"<p>Write <code>void stats(int *a, int n, int *sum, double *avg)</code>. Test on <code>{10, 20, 30, 40}</code> and print <code>sum=100 avg=25.0</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid stats(int *a, int n, int *sum, double *avg) {\n    *sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        *sum += *(a + i);\n    }\n    *avg = (double)(*sum) / n;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40}, s;\n    double av;\n    stats(a, 4, &s, &av);\n    printf(\"sum=%d avg=%.1f\\n\", s, av);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","checklist":["Initializes <code>*sum = 0</code> inside <code>stats</code>","Computes <code>*avg = (double)(*sum) / n</code>","Passes <code>&amp;s, &amp;av</code> from <code>main</code>"]},{"id":"c-15-01","diff":"easy","kind":"run","title":"String length without strlen","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>Read a single word (no spaces) into <code>char s[50]</code> and print its length using a loop until <code>'\\0'</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char s[50];\n    scanf(\"%s\", s);\n    int len = 0;\n    while (s[len] != '\\0') len++;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", len);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"Istanbul","hint":"Count characters while <code>s[len] != '\\0'</code>."},{"id":"c-15-02","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix string comparison with ==","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>Fix the bug so two identical words print <code>Same</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char a[30], b[30];\n    scanf(\"%s %s\", a, b);\n    if (strcmp(a, b) == 0) printf(\"Same\\n\");\n    else printf(\"Different\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char a[30], b[30];\n    scanf(\"%s %s\", a, b);\n    if (a == b) printf(\"Same\\n\");\n    else printf(\"Different\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","stdin":"hello hello","hint":"Use <code>strcmp(a, b) == 0</code>."},{"id":"c-15-03","diff":"easy","kind":"bug","title":"Fix missing '\\0' in manual string","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>This manual char array is missing its null terminator! Fix it so <code>printf(\"%s\\n\", s)</code> prints <code>CAT</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char s[4];\n    s[0] = 'C'; s[1] = 'A'; s[2] = 'T'; s[3] = '\\0';\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char s[4];\n    s[0] = 'C'; s[1] = 'A'; s[2] = 'T';\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Add <code>s[3] = '\\0';</code>."},{"id":"c-15-04","diff":"medium","kind":"fill","title":"Copy and concatenate strings","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks to copy <code>\"Code\"</code> into <code>buf</code> and append <code>\" in C\"</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char buf[30];\n    strcpy(buf, \"Code\");\n    strcat(buf, \" in C\");\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", buf);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char buf[30];\n    ____(buf, \"Code\");\n    ____(buf, \" in C\");\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", buf);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Use <code>strcpy</code> and <code>strcat</code>."},{"id":"c-15-05","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict ASCII and ctype output","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char c = 'a';\n    printf(\"%c %d %d\\n\", toupper(c), isdigit('7') != 0, isalpha('7') != 0);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-15-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Count digits in a string","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>Read a word <code>s</code> and print how many digit characters (<code>'0'..'9'</code>) it contains using <code>isdigit</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char s[100];\n    scanf(\"%s\", s);\n    int cnt = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; s[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        if (isdigit(s[i])) cnt++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", cnt);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"CMP153Fall2026","hint":"Include <code>&lt;ctype.h&gt;</code> and loop until <code>s[i] != '\\0'</code>."},{"id":"c-15-07","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Convert a string to uppercase","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>Read a word and print it in all uppercase using <code>toupper</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char s[100];\n    scanf(\"%s\", s);\n    for (int i = 0; s[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        s[i] = toupper(s[i]);\n    }\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"Gelisim","hint":"Replace each <code>s[i]</code> with <code>toupper(s[i])</code>."},{"id":"c-15-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Count vowels in a line (fgets)","lesson":"c-15","prompt":"<p>Read a full line with <code>fgets</code> and print the count of English vowels (<code>a, e, i, o, u</code>, case-insensitive).</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char line[200];\n    fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin);\n    int v = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; line[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        char c = tolower(line[i]);\n        if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u') v++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", v);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"Computer Programming I","checklist":["Reads spaces safely with <code>fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin)</code>","Normalizes case with <code>tolower</code>","Counts <code>a, e, i, o, u</code> and prints total"]},{"id":"c-16-01","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict width and alignment","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>What does this program print? (Pay attention to spaces!)</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"[%5d][%-5d][%05d]\\n\", 7, 7, 7);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-16-02","diff":"easy","kind":"fill","title":"Format a table row","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>Fill in the <code>____</code> blanks so <code>\"Ali\"</code> is left-aligned in 8 chars and <code>3.5</code> is right-aligned in 6 chars with 2 decimals: <code>[Ali     ][  3.50]</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"[%-8s][%6.2f]\\n\", \"Ali\", 3.5);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"[____][____]\\n\", \"Ali\", 3.5);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"Left-align string: <code>%-8s</code>; 6-wide 2-decimal float: <code>%6.2f</code>."},{"id":"c-16-03","diff":"easy","kind":"predict","title":"Predict printf return value","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int k = printf(\"Hello\");\n    printf(\" %d\\n\", k);\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-16-04","diff":"medium","kind":"bug","title":"Fix field width vs precision mix-up","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>This should print <code>3.14</code> in a 7-character-wide field (<code>   3.14</code>), not <code>3.1400000</code>! Fix the format specifier.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"[%7.2f]\\n\", 3.14159);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"[%2.7f]\\n\", 3.14159);\n    return 0;\n}","hint":"<code>%width.precisionf</code> — width comes first, precision after the dot."},{"id":"c-16-05","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Zero-padded date printer","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>Read <code>day</code>, <code>month</code>, <code>year</code> (e.g. <code>5 9 2026</code>) and print <code>05/09/2026</code>.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int d, m, y;\n    scanf(\"%d %d %d\", &d, &m, &y);\n    printf(\"%02d/%02d/%04d\\n\", d, m, y);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"5 9 2026","hint":"Use <code>%02d</code> for day and month, <code>%04d</code> for year."},{"id":"c-16-06","diff":"medium","kind":"run","title":"Sum integers until sentinel 0","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>Read integers until <code>0</code> is entered (<code>while (scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) == 1 &amp;&amp; x != 0)</code>) and print their sum.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x, sum = 0;\n    while (scanf(\"%d\", &x) == 1 && x != 0) {\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"5 10 15 20 0","hint":"Check both <code>scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) == 1</code> and <code>x != 0</code> so the loop stops at <code>0</code>."},{"id":"c-16-07","diff":"medium","kind":"predict","title":"Predict string precision %.4s","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>What does this program print?</p>","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    printf(\"[%.4s][%6.3s]\\n\", \"Programming\", \"Programming\");\n    return 0;\n}"},{"id":"c-16-08","diff":"exam","kind":"run","title":"Formatted grade report table","lesson":"c-16","prompt":"<p>Read 2 student names and scores (<code>Deniz 88 Ece 95</code>) and print a header and 2 rows with name left-aligned in 10 chars and score right-aligned in 5 chars.</p>","solution":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char n1[30], n2[30];\n    int s1, s2;\n    scanf(\"%s %d %s %d\", n1, &s1, n2, &s2);\n    printf(\"%-10s %5s\\n\", \"Name\", \"Score\");\n    printf(\"%-10s %5d\\n\", n1, s1);\n    printf(\"%-10s %5d\\n\", n2, s2);\n    return 0;\n}","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    // write your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","stdin":"Deniz 88 Ece 95","checklist":["Reads names and scores cleanly","Uses <code>%-10s</code> for left-aligned names and <code>%5d</code> for right-aligned scores","Prints aligned header and rows"]}],"challenges":[],"cheat":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-1-program-skeleton\"><h2 id=\"1-program-skeleton\">1. Program skeleton</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    /* statements */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-data-types-printf-scanf-specifiers\"><h2 id=\"2-data-types-printf-scanf-specifiers\">2. Data types &amp; <code>printf</code> / <code>scanf</code> specifiers</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Typical size</th><th><code>printf</code></th><th><code>scanf</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>4 bytes</td><td><code>%d</code></td><td><code>%d</code> (needs <code>&amp;x</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>long</code></td><td>4–8 bytes</td><td><code>%ld</code></td><td><code>%ld</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>4 bytes</td><td><code>%f</code>, <code>%.2f</code></td><td><code>%f</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>double</code></td><td>8 bytes</td><td><code>%f</code>, <code>%.2f</code></td><td><code>%lf</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>char</code></td><td>1 byte</td><td><code>%c</code></td><td><code>\" %c\"</code> (leading space skips <code>\\n</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>char[]</code> (string)</td><td><code>len + 1</code></td><td><code>%s</code></td><td><code>%19s</code> (no <code>&amp;</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-operators-precedence-high-low\"><h2 id=\"3-operators-precedence-high-low\">3. Operators &amp; precedence (high → low)</h2>\n<ol><li><code>()</code> <code>[]</code> <code>-&gt;</code> <code>.</code></li><li>Unary: <code>!</code> <code>++</code> <code>--</code> <code>(type)</code> <code>&amp;</code> <code>*</code> <code>-</code></li><li><code>*</code> <code>/</code> <code>%</code> (integer <code>/</code> truncates toward 0; <code>7 / 2 == 3</code>, <code>7 % 2 == 1</code>)</li><li><code>+</code> <code>-</code></li><li><code>&lt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&gt;=</code></li><li><code>==</code> <code>!=</code> (never use <code>=</code> inside <code>if</code> by mistake!)</li><li><code>&amp;&amp;</code> (AND)</li><li><code>||</code> (OR)</li><li><code>?:</code> (ternary)</li><li><code>=</code> <code>+=</code> <code>-=</code> <code>*=</code> <code>/=</code> <code>%=</code></li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-control-flow\"><h2 id=\"4-control-flow\">4. Control flow</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>if (cond) { ... } else if (cond2) { ... } else { ... }\n\nswitch (x) {\n    case 1: ...; break;\n    default: ...;\n}\n\nfor (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { ... }   // known count\nwhile (cond) { ... }                   // 0 or more times\ndo { ... } while (cond);               // at least 1 time (note semicolon!)</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-5-arrays-strings-functions-pointers\"><h2 id=\"5-arrays-strings-functions-pointers\">5. Arrays, strings, functions &amp; pointers</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Arrays</strong>: 0-indexed (<code>a[0]</code> .. <code>a[n-1]</code>). Passing <code>a</code> to a function passes a pointer to <code>a[0]</code> (also pass <code>n</code>!).</li><li><strong>Strings</strong>: <code>char s[20] = \"hello\";</code> ends with <code>'\\0'</code>. <code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code>: <code>strlen(s)</code>, <code>strcpy(dst, src)</code>, <code>strcmp(a, b) == 0</code>, <code>strcat(dst, src)</code>.</li><li><strong>Pointers</strong>: <code>int *p = &amp;x;</code> → <code>p</code> holds address of <code>x</code>, <code>*p</code> reads/writes <code>x</code>.</li><li><strong>Pass by reference</strong>: <code>void swap(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }</code> called as <code>swap(&amp;x, &amp;y);</code>.</li><li><strong>2D array</strong>: <code>int m[R][C];</code> → <code>m[r][c]</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-6-structs-file-i-o\"><h2 id=\"6-structs-file-i-o\">6. Structs &amp; File I/O</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>struct Student {\n    char name[30];\n    int id;\n    double gpa;\n};\nstruct Student s = {\"Ali\", 101, 3.45};\nstruct Student *p = &amp;s;\np-&gt;gpa = 3.60;  // same as (*p).gpa</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>FILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\"); // \"w\" write/overwrite, \"a\" append\nif (fp == NULL) return 1;\nfprintf(fp, \"%d\\n\", x);\nfscanf(fp, \"%d\", &amp;x);\nfclose(fp);</code></pre>\n</section>","title":"Algorithms & Programming (C) — Cheat Sheet"},"mocks":{"midterm":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-question-1-20-pts-trace-predict-output\"><h2 id=\"question-1-20-pts-trace-predict-output\">Question 1 (20 pts) — Trace &amp; predict output</h2>\n<p>Trace the program by hand and write its exact output:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 11, b = 4;\n    int q = a / b;\n    int r = a % b;\n    double d = (double)a / b;\n    printf(\"q=%d r=%d d=%.2f\\n\", q, r, d);\n\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {\n        if (i % 2 == 0) continue;\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    printf(\"sum=%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>q=2 r=3 d=2.75\nsum=9</code></pre>\n<ul><li><code>11 / 4</code> is integer division → <code>2</code>, <code>11 % 4</code> → <code>3</code>.</li><li><code>(double)11 / 4</code> promotes to <code>double</code> → <code>2.75</code>.</li><li>Loop adds odd <code>i</code> values <code>1 + 3 + 5 = 9</code> (<code>continue</code> skips <code>2</code> and <code>4</code>).</li></ul></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 1 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Correctly computes <code>q=2</code>, <code>r=3</code> (integer division and modulo)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Correctly computes <code>d=2.75</code> with 2 decimal places</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Correctly traces <code>continue</code> to get <code>sum=9</code></span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-2-25-pts-conditionals-switch\"><h2 id=\"question-2-25-pts-conditionals-switch\">Question 2 (25 pts) — Conditionals &amp; switch</h2>\n<p>Write a C program that reads an integer <code>score</code> (<code>0</code> to <code>100</code>) and prints:</p>\n<ul><li><code>\"Invalid\"</code> if <code>score &lt; 0</code> or <code>score &gt; 100</code></li><li><code>\"AA\"</code> if <code>score &gt;= 90</code></li><li><code>\"BA\"</code> if <code>80 &lt;= score &lt; 90</code></li><li><code>\"BB\"</code> if <code>70 &lt;= score &lt; 80</code></li><li><code>\"CB\"</code> if <code>60 &lt;= score &lt; 70</code></li><li><code>\"FF\"</code> if <code>score &lt; 60</code></li></ul>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int score;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &amp;score);\n\n    if (score &lt; 0 || score &gt; 100) {\n        printf(\"Invalid\\n\");\n    } else if (score &gt;= 90) {\n        printf(\"AA\\n\");\n    } else if (score &gt;= 80) {\n        printf(\"BA\\n\");\n    } else if (score &gt;= 70) {\n        printf(\"BB\\n\");\n    } else if (score &gt;= 60) {\n        printf(\"CB\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"FF\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 2 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Checks out-of-range (<code>&lt; 0 || &gt; 100</code>) first</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <code>else if</code> chain in descending order (avoids chained <code>60 &lt;= score &lt; 70</code> bug)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <code>&amp;score</code> in <code>scanf</code></span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-3-25-pts-loops-sentinel-input\"><h2 id=\"question-3-25-pts-loops-sentinel-input\">Question 3 (25 pts) — Loops &amp; sentinel input</h2>\n<p>Write a C program that keeps reading integers until the user enters <code>0</code> (sentinel). Print how many positive integers were entered and their average (<code>0.00</code> if none were positive). Ignore negative numbers in the average.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x, count = 0, sum = 0;\n    while (scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x) == 1 &amp;&amp; x != 0) {\n        if (x &gt; 0) {\n            sum += x;\n            count++;\n        }\n    }\n    if (count == 0) {\n        printf(\"count=0 avg=0.00\\n\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"count=%d avg=%.2f\\n\", count, (double)sum / count);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 3 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Stops cleanly when <code>0</code> is read</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Counts and sums only positive numbers (<code>x &gt; 0</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Guards against division by zero when <code>count == 0</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Casts <code>sum</code> to <code>double</code> before dividing</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-4-30-pts-1d-arrays-functions\"><h2 id=\"question-4-30-pts-1d-arrays-functions\">Question 4 (30 pts) — 1D arrays &amp; functions</h2>\n<p>Write a function <code>int countAbove(const int a[], int n, int limit)</code> that returns how many elements in <code>a</code> are strictly greater than <code>limit</code>. Then write a <code>main</code> that tests it on <code>{12, 5, 19, 8, 25, 14}</code> with <code>limit = 10</code> and prints the result.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint countAbove(const int a[], int n, int limit) {\n    int c = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        if (a[i] &gt; limit) {\n            c++;\n        }\n    }\n    return c;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int nums[] = {12, 5, 19, 8, 25, 14};\n    int n = 6;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", countAbove(nums, n, 10));  /* prints 4 */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 4 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Correct function signature taking array + size <code>n</code> + <code>limit</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Loops <code>0 &lt;= i &lt; n</code> without out-of-bounds access</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses strict <code>&gt;</code> (<code>a[i] &gt; limit</code>) and returns <code>c</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Calls <code>countAbove</code> from <code>main</code> and prints <code>4</code></span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>","title":"Algorithms & Programming (C) — Midterm Mock Exam (Weeks 1–6)","minutes":90},"final":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-question-1-20-pts-pointers-trace\"><h2 id=\"question-1-20-pts-pointers-trace\">Question 1 (20 pts) — Pointers &amp; trace</h2>\n<p>Trace the program and write its exact output:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid bump(int *p, int val) {\n    *p += val;\n    val += 100;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    int *ptr = &amp;x;\n    *ptr = 15;\n    bump(&amp;x, y);\n    printf(\"x=%d y=%d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>x=35 y=20</code></pre>\n<ul><li><code>*ptr = 15;</code> changes <code>x</code> from <code>10</code> to <code>15</code>.</li><li><code>bump(&amp;x, y)</code> passes the address of <code>x</code> and a copy of <code>y</code> (<code>20</code>).</li><li><code>*p += 20</code> changes <code>x</code> from <code>15</code> to <code>35</code>.</li><li><code>val += 100</code> only changes the local parameter <code>val</code>; <code>y</code> stays <code>20</code>.</li></ul></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 1 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Tracks <code>*ptr = 15</code> updating <code>x</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Tracks <code>*p += val</code> updating <code>x</code> to <code>35</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Recognizes <code>val</code> is passed by value so <code>y</code> stays <code>20</code></span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-2-20-pts-strings-without-string-h\"><h2 id=\"question-2-20-pts-strings-without-string-h\">Question 2 (20 pts) — Strings without <code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code></h2>\n<p>Write a function <code>int countVowels(const char s[])</code> that counts how many lowercase vowels (<code>a, e, i, o, u</code>) appear in string <code>s</code>, stopping at <code>'\\0'</code>. Do not use <code>strlen</code>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>int countVowels(const char s[]) {\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; s[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        char c = s[i];\n        if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u') {\n            count++;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}</code></pre></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 2 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Loops until <code>s[i] != '\\0'</code> without calling <code>strlen</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Checks all 5 vowels accurately</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Returns the integer count</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-3-30-pts-2d-arrays-matrix-row-sums\"><h2 id=\"question-3-30-pts-2d-arrays-matrix-row-sums\">Question 3 (30 pts) — 2D arrays (matrix row sums)</h2>\n<p>Write a function <code>void rowSums(int mat[][4], int rows, int out[])</code> that stores the sum of each row <code>r</code> (<code>0 .. rows-1</code>) into <code>out[r]</code>. Test it in <code>main</code> with a <code>2x4</code> matrix.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid rowSums(int mat[][4], int rows, int out[]) {\n    for (int r = 0; r &lt; rows; r++) {\n        int s = 0;\n        for (int c = 0; c &lt; 4; c++) {\n            s += mat[r][c];\n        }\n        out[r] = s;\n    }\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int m[2][4] = {\n        {1, 2, 3, 4},\n        {10, 20, 30, 40}\n    };\n    int sums[2];\n    rowSums(m, 2, sums);\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", sums[0], sums[1]);  /* 10 100 */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 3 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Declares column size <code>[][4]</code> in parameter list</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Resets <code>s = 0</code> inside the outer <code>r</code> loop</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Writes <code>out[r] = s</code> and prints <code>10 100</code></span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-question-4-30-pts-structs-file-i-o\"><h2 id=\"question-4-30-pts-structs-file-i-o\">Question 4 (30 pts) — Structs &amp; File I/O</h2>\n<p>Define a <code>struct Product</code> with <code>int id</code>, <code>char name[32]</code>, and <code>double price</code>. Write a <code>main</code> function that reads <code>n</code> products from <code>\"products.txt\"</code> (format: <code>id name price</code> per line) into an array <code>struct Product items[100]</code> and prints the name of the most expensive product. Close the file properly.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstruct Product {\n    int id;\n    char name[32];\n    double price;\n};\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *fp = fopen(\"products.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (fp == NULL) {\n        printf(\"Cannot open file\\n\");\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    struct Product items[100];\n    int n = 0;\n    while (n &lt; 100 &amp;&amp;\n           fscanf(fp, \"%d %31s %lf\", &amp;items[n].id, items[n].name, &amp;items[n].price) == 3) {\n        n++;\n    }\n    fclose(fp);\n\n    if (n == 0) {\n        printf(\"No products\\n\");\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        if (items[i].price &gt; items[best].price) {\n            best = i;\n        }\n    }\n    printf(\"Most expensive: %s (%.2f)\\n\", items[best].name, items[best].price);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Question 4 marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Defines <code>struct Product</code> with <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>price</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Checks <code>fp == NULL</code> after <code>fopen</code> and calls <code>fclose(fp)</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <code>fscanf</code> return value <code>== 3</code> and bounds <code>n &lt; 100</code>, with <code>%31s</code> and <code>&amp;items[n].price</code> (<code>%lf</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Correctly finds and prints the maximum-price product</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>","title":"Algorithms & Programming (C) — Final Mock Exam (Comprehensive)","minutes":120}},"extra":{},"essentials":{"html":"<p>Programming is giving a computer <strong>exact steps</strong>. Eight pictures, eight ideas.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">From code to program</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-pipeline\"></div>\n<p>Plan the steps first — a <strong>flowchart</strong> draws them with these shapes:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-symbols\"></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/c-02\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Every program has the same skeleton</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-skeleton\"></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/c-05\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Variables are labelled boxes</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-variables\"></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/c-05\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">scanf reads, printf shows</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-io\"></div>\n<p>Format codes: <code>%d</code> int · <code>%f</code> float · <code>%lf</code> double (scanf) · <code>%c</code> char · <code>%s</code> text. <strong>Never forget the <code>&amp;</code> in scanf.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\" data-stdin=\"20\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint main() {\n    int age;\n    printf(\"How old are you? \");\n    scanf(\"%d\", &amp;age);\n    printf(\"Next year you'll be %d.\\n\", age + 1);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/c-06\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">if chooses a path</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-ifelse\"></div>\n<p><code>==</code> compares, <code>=</code> stores — mixing them up is the classic exam bug. <a href=\"#/lesson/c-08\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Loops repeat</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"flow\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">s  start  Start\na  proc  i = 1\nc  dec  i &lt;= 5 ?  yes:p no:z\np  io  print i\nk  proc  i = i + 1  -&gt;c\nz  end  End</pre></div>\n<p><code>for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++)</code> = exactly this picture. Press <strong>Run</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++)\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/c-09\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Functions are machines</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-function\"></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/c-11\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Arrays and strings are rows of boxes</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-array\"></div>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-strings\"></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/c-12\">Go deeper: arrays</a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/c-15\">strings</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Pointers hold addresses</div><div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"viz\" data-name=\"c-pointer\"></div>\n<p>That's why <code>scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x)</code> can fill <code>x</code>. <a href=\"#/lesson/c-13\">Go deeper</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-remember\"><div class=\"callout-h\">If you remember only this</div><ul><li><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;</code> · <code>int main() { … return 0; }</code> · <code>;</code> after every statement</li><li><code>int</code> / <code>double</code> / <code>char</code> — int ÷ int drops the decimals</li><li><code>scanf</code> needs <code>&amp;</code> · <code>==</code> compares, <code>=</code> stores</li><li>arrays start at <code>[0]</code> · <code>&amp;</code> = where? · <code>*</code> = what's there?</li></ul></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does <code>printf(\"%d\", 7 / 2);</code> print?","opts":[{"h":"3.5","why":"both numbers are int, so the decimal part is dropped"},{"h":"3","ok":1,"why":"integer division"},{"h":"4","why":"C doesn't round up here"},{"h":"3.0","why":"%d prints an int"}],"why":"int / int gives an int."},{"q":"Which line correctly reads an integer into <code>n</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>scanf(\"%d\", n);</code>","why":"the &amp; is missing"},{"h":"<code>scanf(\"%d\", &amp;n);</code>","ok":1,"why":"&amp; gives scanf the address of n"},{"h":"<code>scanf(n);</code>","why":"needs a format string"},{"h":"<code>printf(\"%d\", &amp;n);</code>","why":"printf shows, it doesn't read"}],"why":"scanf needs the address: <code>&amp;n</code>."},{"q":"<code>int a[4] = {3, 6, 9, 12};</code> — what is <code>a[1]</code>?","opts":[{"h":"3","why":"that's a[0]"},{"h":"6","ok":1,"why":"indexes start at 0"},{"h":"9","why":"that's a[2]"},{"h":"error","why":"a[1] exists"}],"why":"First element = index 0."},{"q":"What does <code>if (x = 5)</code> do?","opts":[{"h":"compares x with 5","why":"that needs =="},{"h":"stores 5 in x (and the condition is always true)","ok":1,"why":"the classic = vs == bug"},{"h":"causes a compile error","why":"it compiles, which is why it's dangerous"},{"h":"does nothing","why":"it changes x"}],"why":"Compare with <code>==</code>."},{"q":"<code>int x = 1; int *p = &amp;x; *p = 4;</code> What is x now?","opts":[{"h":"1","why":"*p changed it"},{"h":"4","ok":1,"why":"p points to x, so *p = 4 changes x"},{"h":"the address of x","why":"x stores a number, not an address"},{"h":"an error","why":"this is valid C"}],"why":"<code>*p</code> means \"the variable p points to\"."}]],"title":"Programming in C — the essentials","minutes":20,"intro":"variables, input/output, if, loops, functions, arrays, pointers"}});
